\par\par \b \uc2 Indian Rupee Hits 7-Mo High Vs Dlr; Govt Bonds Higher  \b0\par\par\uc2 589 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 November 2004\par 14:22\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI (Dow Jones)--The Indian rupee extended its rally against the dollar Tuesday, hitting a seven-month high as surging local share prices fueled hopes that strong capital inflows will persist.  \par \par \uc2 Broad dollar weakness and scaled-back \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  underscored the rupee's strength. The Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been a dollar buyer in recent weeks, but has significantly slowed down its purchases since Monday, traders said.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar closed at INR44.63 Tuesday, a level last tested in early May. It ended at INR44.82 Monday.  \par \par \uc2 "The stock market rally has driven today's \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  trend. With Indian equity still looking good to buy, foreigners are going to pump more money here," said a trader at a local bank.  \par \par \uc2 The key Indian share index, the Bombay Stock Exchange Sensitive Index closed at a record high of 6234.29 points on heavy inflows of foreign portfolio investment. Such inflows have reached a record high of $6.93 billion so far in 2004, beating the previous record of $6.59 billion for the whole of 2003.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts expect dollar weakness and increased capital inflows into the stock market to help the rupee rise further in coming months.  \par \par \uc2 Siddharth Mathur, head of research at J.P. Morgan in Mumbai, forecasts that the dollar will extend its slide to INR44.30 by the end of 2004, and to INR43.20 by mid-2005.  \par \par \uc2 Dollar forward rates edged up Tuesday, despite the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's fall on the spot market, on buying by state-owned banks. The one-year forward contract ended at a 0.77% premium to the spot rate, up from 0.66% at the previous close.  \par \par \uc2 Mifor-based interest rate swaps ended slightly higher Tuesday, tracking forward-dollar premiums. The two-year Mifor swap hardened to 4.30%-4.35% from 4.18%-4.23% at the previous close, while the five-year swap rose to 5.10%-5.15% from 5.00%-5.05%.  \par \par \uc2 Government bond prices ended slightly higher Tuesday on scattered bargain hunting after Monday's decline. The actively traded 7.55% 2010 bond ended at INR103.20, up from INR103.05 at the previous close. Its yield edged lower to 6.83% from 6.87%.  \par \par \uc2 Bonds could drift lower in the coming sessions due to weak demand as traders await details of a federal government bond auction due in the first week of December.  \par \par \uc2 The overnight call money rate closed unchanged at 4.70%-4.80% in adequate money market liquidity. [ 30-11-04 1322GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Rupee Vs Dollar Close \par Tuesday         Previous \par INR44.6300      INR44.8200 \par  \par Forward Dollar Premiums/Discounts \par January                April                   October \par INR0.055 Vs INR0.035   INR0.180 Vs INR0.125    INR0.33 Vs INR0.28 \par  \par Daily Overnight Reverse Repurchase Auction Amount \par Tuesday         Previous \par INR56.05 Bln    INR49.20 Bln \par  \par Reverse Repurchase Auction Rate \par Tuesday         Previous \par 4.75%           4.75% \par  \par Call Rate \par Tuesday         Previous Close \par 4.70%-4.80%     4.70%-4.80% \par  \par Interest Rate Swaps  Current Price   Previous Price \par  2-Year Mifor swap   4.30%-4.35%     4.18%-4.23% \par  5-Year Mifor swap   5.10%-5.15%     5.00%-5.05% \par  5-Year OIS          6.43%-6.47%     6.45%-6.49% \par  \par Benchmark Bond Prices \par Security   Maturity   Current Price   Previous Price \par  7.55% GOI   2010        103.20          103.05 \par  6.65% GOI   2009         99.40           99.30 \par    \f28 \par \par \