\par\par \b \uc2 Indian rupee seen gaining, cbank likely to check.  \b0\par\par\uc2 336 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 November 2004\par 04:23\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     BOMBAY, Nov 22 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee is expected to\par open stronger on Monday as the dollar wallows near record lows\par overseas after receiving little support from a weekend meeting\par of the Group of 20 (G20) nations, traders said.\par     But dealers said gains were likely to be crimped by a\par watchful \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , which has stoutly prevented the rupee\par from appreciating past the 45 per dollar mark in recent\par sessions.\par     "The lack of any specific statements by the G20 supporting\par the dollar should help drive more dollar selling by exporters\par early in the session,"  a chief dealer at a European bank said.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     "But we expect the RBI (Reserve Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 India\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ) to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par strongly and so we should see a choppy session with trading\par likely in a 45 to 45.20 range."\par     The Indian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  yanked the rupee back from session\par highs above 45 thrice in three consecutive days last week,\par helping snap a six-day rally in the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on Thursday\par and trimming its gains in the week to 0.12 percent.\par     The RBI's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , coming amid a strong upsurge in\par foreign capital inflows, have helped boost the country's\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves to a record of $123.5 billion as on\par Nov. 12. Additions to the kitty in 2004 are now at a robust $23\par billion.\par     Traders said some month-end demand from importers could\par also exert some pressure on the rupee but other factors,\par including comments from the Indian Finance Minister indicating\par little concern about the dollar's slide, would support the\par rupee.\par  \par     For the full-text of a Reuters interview with Finance\par Minister Palaniappan Chidambaram, please\par             .\par     The rupee          ended Friday at 45.0950/1050 per dollar,\par marginally lower than Thursday's 45.0750/0850 close.\par -------------------------------------------------------------\par                               MARKET SNAPSHOT\par                                Previous close\par     Bombay Sensitive Index (pts)     5,961.71\par     Indian rupee (/$ )           45.0950/1050\par     7.37 percent 2014 bond (%)         7.0880\par     Call money (%)                  4.75/5.00\par     6-mth fwd annualised premium (%)     1.76\par -------------------------------------------------------------.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041122e0bm002mi\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
