\par\par \b \uc2 Indian rupee ends steady at 5-month highs, fwds drop.  \b0\par\par\uc2 433 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 November 2004\par 12:09\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     BOMBAY, Nov 9 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee ended steady\par around five-month highs on Tuesday, with corporates stepping up\par dollar purchases and traders wary about increased \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to curb sharp gains by the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par     Underlying sentiment was positive, helped by robust foreign\par fund inflows, a broadly weak dollar and on increased\par speculation about a revaluation of the Chinese yuan.\par     The rupee          ended at 45.1700/1775 per dollar, off an\par intra-day high of 45.08 and barely changed from its previous\par close of 45.1700/1800 -- the strongest since June 11, when it\par ended at 45.1550/1650.\par     "There was some corporate dollar buying and possibly\par purchases by foreign funds to make remittances," said a dealer\par at a state-run bank. "But with regional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  showing an\par appreciating bias, more gains are likely in store for the\par rupee."\par  \par     But traders were wary of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the Reserve Bank\par of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 India\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     The Indian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  bid aggressively for dollars on\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Monday to curb sharp gains by the rupee, which touched 44.87  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 per dollar - its highest since May - before giving up most of  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 the gains to end at a new five-month high.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The rupee is now up nearly one percent so far this calendar  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 year, rebounding from a 6.3 percent slump between April and  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 July, when foreign investment inflows had slowed after a  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 left-backed government took office in New Delhi and the U.S.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve started to raise interest rates.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investments from foreign funds have since revived as  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 investors bet on robust economic growth. Foreign funds have  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 bought nearly $6 billion worth of Indian shares so far this  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 year, after a record $6.7 billion in the whole of 2003.  \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 India\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  often \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervenes\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign  \f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market through state-run banks to curb sharp swings.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Monday's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would lead to a  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 slight easing in rupee liquidity in the next few days.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Premiums on the forward dollar eased on a firm outlook for  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and as exporters stepped in to encash  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 receivables. The six-month forward ended at an annualised 2.26  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 percent, down from the previous close of 2.68 percent.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par ------------------------------------------------------------\par                                MARKET SNAPSHOT\par                              