\par\par \b \uc2 Indian rupee at fresh 5-month high despite c.bank.  \b0\par\par\uc2 474 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 November 2004\par 12:10\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     BOMBAY, Nov 8 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee slid from\par intra-day peaks after state-run banks, possibly acting on\par behalf of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , stepped in to curb sharp gains, but\par the local unit still managed to end at a new 5-month high on\par Monday.\par     The rupee          ended at 45.1700/1800 per dollar, off an\par intra-day peak of 44.87 hit in stray deals in the morning and\par marginally up from Friday's close of 45.2000/2200. It last\par ended firmer than this on June 11, when it closed at\par 45.1550/1650.\par     "The constant dollar buying by state-run banks drove down\par the rupee and squeezed traders who had gone short," said a\par dealer at a foreign bank. "Their dollar-buying spree is\par probably aimed at adding rupee liquidity and lowering\par inter-bank call rates."\par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 India\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  often \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervenes\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign\par exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market through state-run banks to curb sharp swings by\par the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Monday's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par would lead to a slight easing in rupee liquidity in the next\par few days.\par     A cash crunch in the money market has seen call money\par   trade at around 6 percent levels, the highest since\par June. It usually straddles the reverse repo rate of 4.75\par percent when liquidity in the banking system is adequate.\par     Earlier, the rupee strengthened to 44.87, helped by a weak\par dollar, which hit record lows against the euro, increased\par speculation about a revaluation of the Chinese yuan and a drop\par in prices of crude oil, India's biggest import.\par     The dollar hit a record low against the euro and a\par multi-month trough against the yen on worries about bloated\par deficits in the United States.\par     Oil prices further moved away from recent record highs as\par easing concerns about winter supplies prompted big-money hedge\par funds to switch money away from oil and into other financial\par markets.\par     The rupee is now up nearly 1 percent so far this calendar\par year, rebounding from a 6.3 percent slump between April and\par July, when foreign investment inflows slowed after a\par communist-backed government took office in New Delhi and the\par U.S. Federal Reserve started to raise interest rates.\par     But foreign funds investment has since revived as investors\par bet on robust economic growth. Foreign funds have bought $5.8\par billion worth of Indian shares so far this year, after a record\par $6.7 billion in the whole of 2003.\par  \par ------------------------------------------------------------\par                                MARKET SNAPSHOT\par                               Monday's close      Previous\par close\par     Bombay Sensitive Index (pts)    5,930.47           5,891.36\par     Indian rupee (/$ )          45.1700/1800       45.2000/2200\par     7.37 percent 2014 bond (%)        7.3095*            7.2212\par     Call money (%)                 5.90/6.10          5.75/6.00\par     6-mth fwd annualised premium (%)    2.68\par 2.79\par  \par ------------------------------------------------------------.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041108e0b800ecv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgo