\par\par \b \uc2 Indian rupee slips as record oil prices weigh.  \b0\par\par\uc2 423 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Oktober 2004\par 13:20\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     BOMBAY, Oct 12 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee lost more\par ground on Tuesday, retreating from last week's three-month\par peak, with sentiment edgy on record-high crude oil prices which\par analysts say will expand India's already-widening trade\par deficit.\par     The rupee          ended at 45.8725/8775 per dollar, off an\par intra-day low of 45.8950, but weaker than Monday's\par 45.8425/8475. The rupee gained 0.2 percent last week to end at\par a three-month closing high of 45.8050/8150 on Friday.\par     "There was sustained dollar demand from the oil companies\par and other large corporates," said a trader at a state-run bank.\par "But I don't think there was support from the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for\par the rupee."\par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 India\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  often \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervenes\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par market through state-run banks to curb excessive swings. In\par recent sessions, it has often been selling dollars from its war\par chest of nearly $119 billion in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves to\par support the rupee and cushion the economy from the price of\par oil.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     Surging oil prices boosted India's trade deficit to nearly\par $10 billion in the April-August period from $6.62 billion a\par year earlier, putting downward pressure on the rupee, which has\par lost nearly 5 percent since it struck a 51-month high in April.\par     Prices of oil, India's top import, surged to new highs on\par Tuesday as supply outages weighed. U.S. crude  set a\par record $54.45 a barrel, marking a sixth successive day of new\par peaks, before easing to $53.94, up 30 cents on the day. In\par London, Brent crude  hit $51.50, before retracing to\par $51, up 34 cents.\par     Analysts say oil at such prices could shave nearly a\par percentage point from India's gross domestic product -- a\par factor that could disrupt steady foreign investment in Indian\par equities.\par     Foreign funds have so far pumped nearly $240 million into\par Indian shares in the first week of October, after investing a\par net $600 million last month.\par     Their investment in local shares is expected to rise ahead\par of strong corporate earnings and a billion-dollar initial\par public offer by National Thermal Power Corp.\par  \par -------------------------------------------------------------\par                                MARKET SNAPSHOT\par                                 Tuesday's close    Monday's\par close\par     Bombay Sensitive Index (pts)       5,676.73\par 5,717.54\par     Indian rupee (/$ )             45.8725/8775\par 45.8425/8475\par     7.37 percent 2014 bond (%)           6.7991\par 6.7920\par     Call money (%)                    4.25/4.50\par 4.25/4.50\par     6-mth fwd annualised premium (% )      2.63\par 2.66\par -------------------------------------------------------------.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041012e0ac008y9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
