\par\par \b \uc2 Indian rupee slips on high oil, cbank eyed.  \b0\par\par\uc2 418 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Oktober 2004\par 06:27\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     BOMBAY, Oct 12 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee retreated\par further on Tuesday morning from recent three-month peaks, with\par sentiment subdued by record high crude oil prices that are\par expected to adversely impact the country's widening trade\par deficit.\par     But traders expect \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par     Foreign fund inflows into local shares on expectations of\par strong corporate earnings and after a billion-dollar initial\par public offer by National Thermal Power Corp. should also offer\par some respite to the rupee.\par     At 9:25 a.m. (0355 GMT), the rupee          was quoted at\par 45.8800/8900 per dollar, weaker than Monday's 45.8425/8475. The\par rupee rose 0.2 percent last week to end at a three-month\par closing high of 45.8050/8150 on Friday.\par     "There seems to be some residual dollar demand from\par corporates which is weighing on the rupee," said a chief trader\par at a private bank.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  "But we have to see at what level the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  comes in to defend the rupee."\par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 India\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  often \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervenes\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par market through state-run banks to curb excessive swings. In\par recent sessions it has often been selling dollars from its war\par chest of nearly $119 billion in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves to support the\par rupee and cushion the economy from record high crude oil\par prices.\par     Surging oil prices helped expand India's trade deficit to\par nearly $10 billion in the April-August period from $6.62\par billion a year earlier, putting downward pressure on the rupee.\par     Prices of oil, India's biggest import, stayed near record\par high levels on Tuesday due to supply worries. Brent crude was\par around $50.55 a barrel, after ending up almost $1 a day earlier\par and around $20 higher than the start of the year.\par     Analysts say that, if crude oil prices stayed at such high\par levels, it could shave nearly a percentage point from India's\par gross domestic product -- a factor that could impact foreign\par fund inflows into India's stock market.\par     Foreign funds have so far pumped nearly $240 million into\par Indian shares in the first week of October, after investing\par about $600 million last month.\par  \par -------------------------------------------------------------\par                                MARKET SNAPSHOT\par                                     9:25 a.m.     Monday's\par close\par     Bombay Sensitive Index (pts)   to open             5,717.54\par     Indian rupee (/$ )          45.8800/8900       45.8425/8475\par     7.37 percent 2014 bond (%)        6.8202             6.7920\par     Call money (%)                 4.40/4.60          4.25/4.50\par     6-mth fwd annualised premium (%) to open               2.66\par -------------------------------------------------------------.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041012e0ac00468\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
