\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS-Wary of inflation, India cbank may let rupee rise.   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Anirban Nag   \par \uc2 656 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 September 2004\par 13:39\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 BOMBAY, Sept 14 (Reuters) - India's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to allow the rupee to rise against the dollar in the near term to try to curb inflationary pressures that threaten growth and stability in Asia's fourth-largest economy, analysts said.   \par \par \uc2 Until a few weeks ago, the Reserve Bank of India (RBI) was selling dollars to check a sharp rupee slide. Now with the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rising, it is unlikely to step in to buy dollars because rupee appreciation makes imports cheaper and the bank's task of combating inflation easier.   \par \par \uc2 "A stronger rupee will help checking inflation a bit," said M.R. Madhavan, strategist at Bank of America, Singapore.   \par \par \uc2 "A 1 percent appreciation in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will lower price levels by about 0.3 percent."   \par \par \uc2 The partially convertible rupee has gained 0.8 percent this week and broke past the key 46 per dollar level to end at a two-month high of 45.9050/9150 on Tuesday - with little indication the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would curb the relatively sharp rise.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has maintained it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb volatility and does not use the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to moderate inflation.   \par \par \uc2 The rupee has risen due to a broadly weaker dollar, hopes of improved foreign capital inflows into a rising stock market and on expectations that domestic inflation has peaked.   \par \par \uc2 India's inflation spiralled to a new 3-1/2-year high of 8.33 percent in the year to Aug. 28. from 4.32 percent in late April prompting the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to announce a 0.5 percent increase in banks' cash reserve ratio (CRR) which will absorb 85 billion rupees from the banking system.   \par \par \uc2 The CRR, or proportion of banks' deposits kept with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , has been raised for the first time in four years.   \par \par \uc2 RBI MEASURE HELPS   \par \par \uc2 The measure announced on Saturday followed duty cuts by the government on fuel and commodities like steel a few weeks ago. Authorities have attributed the spike in inflation mainly to supply-side factors like firm prices of commodities such as crude oil as well as excess cash in the banking system.   \par \par \uc2 "With the decision to raise the reserve ratio, there is a growing perception that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is unlikely \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy dollars as it would add to more rupee liquidity in the system," said U. Venkatraman, head of trading at IDBI Bank said.   \par \par \uc2 "So I expect the rupee to gain to 45.80 in the near term, provided crude oil prices do not spike."   \par \par \uc2 Some analysts say the CRR increase may be the first signal that interest rates are set to rise from three-decade lows. That will likely help the rupee against the dollar as interest rate differentials between the United States and India widen.   \par \par \uc2 The rupee has lost ground since hitting a 51-month high of 43.5450/5500 per dollar on April 7, partly as a result of a slowdown in foreign capital inflows after a surprise change in the Indian government at elections in May.   \par \par \uc2 Rising U.S. interest rates and a spike in prices of crude oil, India's largest import item, have also weighed on the rupee.   \par \par \uc2 Foreign fund inflows are expected to increase on hopes of better earnings from Indian corporates after data showed industrial production rose 7.9 percent in July compared with a 6.6 percent rise last year.   \par \par \uc2 Foreign funds poured in about $4 billion in the first four months of the calendar year, but their investments have slowed since then. They invested $556 million in August and have pumped in nearly $181 million in the first 10 days of September.   \par \par \uc2 Analysts said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's move to allow the rupee to appreciate was unlikely to harm exporters. Exports rose 20 percent in the year to March 2004, even as the rupee appreciated by 9 percent against the dollar.    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040914e09e0073m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
