\par\par \b \uc2 Indian rupee seen rising,foreign fund inflows eyed.  \b0\par\par\uc2 279 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 August 2004\par 05:16\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     BOMBAY, Aug 13 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee is seen rising\par for a fourth straight day, with sentiment upbeat on\par expectations of dollar inflows from foreign funds who have\par successfully bid at the Tata Consultancy Services (TCS) issue.\par     Monday is expected to be the last day for foreign investors\par to pay for their bids in TCS, India's top software services\par exporter, which raised 54.2 billion rupees in Asia's\par second-biggest tech IPO this year.\par  \par     About 60 percent of the issue, India's largest IPO ever,\par was reserved for qualified institutional bidders.\par     Traders said the mood was also upbeat on expectations that\par   \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 India\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was unlikely to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and soak up  \f28 \par \par \uc2 these dollars, allowing the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to gain and possibly  \par \par \uc2 cushion the impact of rising oil prices.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     India imports 70 percent of its crude requirements and oil\par comprises nearly 30 percent of the import bill.\par     A spike in crude oil and global commodity prices led to a\par surge in domestic inflation to a 3-=   high of 7.51 percent,\par prompting the finance minister to say the government and\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would take steps to curb the increase.\par     The rupee          ended Thursday at 46.2000/2100, firmer\par than the previous close of 46.3100/3175 and up nearly 0.35\par percent for the week.\par ---------------------------------