\par\par \b \uc2 Indian rupee rises on suspected cbank  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2 .  \b0\par\par\uc2 364 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 August 2004\par 13:30\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     BOMBAY, Aug 11 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee rose for the\par second straight day on Wednesday on dollar selling by state-run\par banks which usually act on behalf of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , possibly\par to cushion the impact of rising global oil prices, dealers\par said.\par     The rupee          ended at 46.3100/3175 per dollar, off an\par early low of 46.4350 and firmer than the previous close of\par 46.3525/3600.\par     The rupee had eased in early deals after the Federal\par Reserve's quarter percentage point interest rate hike and its\par bullish view on the U.S. economy led to worries about slowing\par foreign capital inflows into \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 India\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par     "State-run banks, which usually \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on behalf of the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , started selling dollars from the 46.36 per dollar\par level," said a chief dealer at a private sector bank. "This led\par to long dollar unwinding by other banks, pushing the rupee up\par towards close."\par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 India\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  usually \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervenes\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  through state-run\par banks to curb excessive volatility in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Steady \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to support the shaky rupee meant \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 India\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves fell by more than $1 billion for a\par second successive week on July 30.\par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves in Asia's fourth-largest economy\par are now down about $2.8 billion from a record $121.11 billion\par on July 16.\par     Traders said \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par market could ease the pressure from soaring oil prices. India\par imports 70 percent of its crude oil requirements and it is the\par country's single biggest import item.\par     A spike in crude oil and commodity prices led to a surge in\par domestic inflation to a three-and-a-half-year high of 7.51\par percent, prompting Finance Minister Palaniappan Chidambaram to\par say the government and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would take steps to curb\par it.\par  \par -------------------------------------------------------------\par                                         MARKET SNAPSHOT\par                             Wednesday's close     Tuesday's\par close\par     Bombay Sensitive Index (pts)   5,175.16\par 5,252.05\par     Indian rupee (/$ )            46.3100/3175\par 46.3525/3600\par     7.37 percent 2014 bond (%)       6.5631\par 6.5224\par    Call money (%)                   4.50/4.75         4.25/4.50\par     6-mth fwd annualised premium (%) 2.80\par 2.56\par -------------------------------------------------------------.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040811e08b008c2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
