\par\par \b \uc2 Indian rupee dips after Fed rate rise, cbank seen.  \b0\par\par\uc2 400 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 August 2004\par 06:42\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     BOMBAY, Aug 11 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee eased early on\par Wednesday after the Federal Reserve's quarter percentage point\par interest rate hike and its bullish view on the U.S. economy led\par to worries of slower foreign capital inflows into India.\par     A rally by the dollar overseas and firm prices of crude\par oil, India's biggest import item, were also weighing on\par sentiment, but dealers said \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was\par curbing sharp losses by the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par     At 9:40 a.m. (0410 GMT), the rupee          was quoted at\par 46.4100/4300 per dollar, off an early low of 46.4350 but still\par weaker than the previous close of 46.3525/3600.\par     "There seems to be some dollar selling by the state banks\par which usually act on behalf of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," said a chief\par dealer at a private bank.\par     "This should help calm nerves in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market."\par     India's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  usually interevenes through state-run\par banks to curb excessive volatility in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Their steady \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to support the shaky rupee has seen\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 India\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves fall by more than $1 billion\par for a second successive week on July 30.\par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves in Asia's fourth-largest economy\par are now down about $2.8 billion from a record $121.11 billion\par on July 16.\par     Traders said the second rate hike in the United States this\par year would see a further slowdown in foreign funds searching\par for high-yielding Indian assets, putting some pressure on the\par rupee.\par     After pumping in an average of more than $1 billion a month\par into Indian assets in the first four months of 2004, foreign\par funds were net sellers of nearly $800 million in May and made\par only relatively small purchases in the next two months.\par     The rupee has lost 6 percent since it struck a 51-month\par high of 43.5450/5500 on April 7, as capital inflows slowed\par after a left-backed government took charge in May and a global\par monetary tightening cycle weighed.\par  \par -------------------------------------------------------------\par                                         MARKET SNAPSHOT\par                                    9:40 a.m.      Tuesday's\par close\par     Bombay Sensitive Index (pts)     to open           5,252.05\par     Indian rupee (/$ )            46.4100/4300\par 46.3525/3600\par     7.37 percent 2014 bond (%)       6.6782\par 6.5224\par    Call money (%)                   4.25/4.50         4.25/4.50\par     6-mth fwd annualised premium (%)   to open\par 2.56\par -------------------------------------------------------------.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040811e08b0048u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
