\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1- \highlight22\uc2 India \highlight\uc2 's reserves fall on cbank  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2 .  \b0\par\par\uc2 490 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Juli 2004\par 10:38\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 BOMBAY, July 31 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves fell by $1.53 billion in the week to July 23, as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  dipped into its war chest to supply scarce dollars and aggressively support a beleaguered rupee, analysts said.  \par \par \uc2 Data released on Saturday showed \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves in Asia's fourth-largest economy fell to $119.58 billion on July 23 from a record high $121.11 billion a week earlier.  \par \par \uc2 For details,  \par \par \uc2 "The fall in reserves definitely reflects the large-scale dollar-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the RBI (Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) that week to calm a panicky market as the rupee depreciated to a year low," said Shubhada Rao, economist at Bank of Baroda.  \par \par \uc2 "While there could have been some revaluation losses in that period, the reserves used by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep the market well supplied with dollars and prevent the rupee from falling too rapidly must account for the major portion of the fall."  \par \par \uc2 Despite the latest week's fall, India's reserves are still up nearly $18 billion in 2004, making it Asia's sixth-largest stockpile.  \par \par \uc2 The week's decline was almost entirely in the foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  assets portion of the reserves, which fell by $1.52 billion. Reserves also include gold, special drawing rights and India's Reserve Tranche Position in the International Monetary Fund.  \par \par \uc2 The RBI said the foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  assets expressed in U.S. dollar terms included the effect of appreciation or depreciation of other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  held in its reserves such as the euro, pound sterling and yen.  \par \par \uc2 The euro depreciated by nearly 3 percent against the dollar in the week ended July 23, after the Federal Reserve chief's upbeat assessment of the U.S. economy bolstered expectations of an interest rate hike, spurring a greenback rally. The Indian rupee, which is only partially convertible, lost 0.77 percent against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  that week to dip to a near 13-month closing low.  \par \par \uc2 The Indian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been under intense pressure in the past two months as foreign capital inflows have slowed on uncertainty over the pace of economic reforms after a Communist-backed government won national elections.  \par \par \uc2 The rupee has lost 6.2 percent since scaling a 51-month closing peak of 43.5450/5500 per dollar on April 7.  \par \par \uc2 Foreign funds' purchases of local assets, which had been a major factor in helping the rupee appreciate until the April peak, have fallen sharply since then.  \par \par \uc2 Although foreign funds' purchase have turned positive in July, at $198.4 million of net investments they are still less than a fifth of the $1 billion average in the first four months of 2004.  \par \par \uc2 That has affected the rupee, which at Friday's close of 46.4350/4550 per dollar was down 1.83 percent against the dollar in 2004, following a record 5.2 percent gain last year.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040731e07v004ph\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
