\par\par \b \uc2 India cbank investments in foreign securities rise.   \b0\par\par\uc2 333 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Juli 2004\par 15:46\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 BOMBAY, July 5 (Reuters) - The Indian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's investments in foreign securities like U.S. Treasury notes rose 30 percent to $35.024 billion in the past year to March 2004, data from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  showed on Monday.   \par \par \uc2 But 40 percent of the $112.959 billion of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves as on March 31, 2004, was deployed as deposits with other central banks and the Bank of International Settlements, the Reserve Bank of India (RBI) said.   \par \par \uc2 The RBI deployed $45.877 billion as deposits with other central banks, up 37 percent from the year earlier.   \par \par \uc2 India's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, currently at $119 billion, were built amid steady \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market as it sought to absorb the robust inflows from foreign funds, expatriate Indians and exports.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  through state-run banks in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to keep exports competitive and prevent sharp swings.   \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  last week said foreign portfolio investment shot up to $11.36 billion in the year to March 2004, compared with $944 million a year earlier helped up an upgrade of the country's soverign rating to investment status by Moody's Investors Service and lured by India's strong economic recovery.   \par \par \uc2 Deposits from expatriate Indians, seeking higher returns on local fixed income assets, rose to $3.6 billion in the year to March 2004, from $3.0 billion a year earlier.   \par \par \uc2 India's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the statement on Monday said that the ratio of short-term debt to \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves declined to 4.2 percent as on March 31, 2004 from 146.5 percent at end March 1991 and 6.1 percent on March 31, 2003.   \par \par \uc2 "Similarly, the ratio of volatile capital inflows (defined to include cumulative portfolio inflows and short-term debt) to reserves declined frm 146.6 percent as at end-March 1991 to 36 percent as at March 2004," the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said.    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040705e075001bt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
