\par\par \b \uc2 India Rupee Falls To 9-Mo Low; Breaks Key 46/Dlr Support  \b0\par\par\uc2 345 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Juni 2004\par 05:37\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI (Dow Jones)--The Indian rupee hit a nine-month low against the dollar in early trading Tuesday, breaking the key INR46 psychological barrier amid expectations that strong corporate dollar buying, which dragged the local unit down Monday, will continue.  \par \par \uc2 Thirty minutes into trading Tuesday, the dollar was at INR46.0700, a level last tested on September 18, 2003.  \par \par \uc2 The rupee closed at INR45.9250 Monday.  \par \par \uc2 Banks heavily bought the dollar as soon as the market opened, expecting the strong corporate demand for the greenback to continue.  \par \par \uc2 "Several companies appear to be securing their near-term import and debt payment needs on fears the dollar could strengthen from current levels," said a trader with a local bank.  \par \par \uc2 Indeed, after a lull that lasted many recent months when the rupee had been strengthening, corporate dollar demand has picked up in recent weeks on the view that the dollar's multiyear fall in international markets may be nearing its end. [ 22-06-04 0418GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Apart from the rising dollar demand from companies, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  arbitrage between the local and the overseas Non-Deliverable Forward markets is also weighing the rupee down.  \par \par \uc2 "The fact that the rupee has fallen more in the NDF market has created an arbitrage opportunity - many banks are buying dollars against rupees in the local market and selling them in the NDF market at a higher rate," said a local treasury head.  \par \par \uc2 In the NDF market, the one-month forward dollar was quoted at INR46.40 Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 Traders expect the rupee to extend losses Tuesday if corporate dollar buying continues as expected. However, a further sharp fall is likely to be prevented by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  - the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  may sell dollars through state-owned banks to support the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 "The next support for the rupee could be INR46.25," says a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with a local bank.  \par \par \uc2 -By Anil Varma, Dow Jones Newswires; 9122-2288-4212; anil.varma@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Shishir Mudgil [ 22-06-04 0434GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040622e06m0002c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
