\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 India \highlight\uc2  rupee recoups as cbank  \highlight22\uc2 intervenes \highlight\uc2 , fwds rise.  \b0\par\par\uc2 315 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Mai 2004\par 10:28\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     BOMBAY, May 27 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee recouped most\par of its early losses by Thursday early afternoon as state-run\par banks sold dollars, most probably on behalf of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central\par bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , to help support the weakening local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , dealers\par said.\par     At 1:45 p.m., the rupee          was at 45.39/41 per\par dollar, well off a trough of 45.55 and almost level with its\par previous close of 45.39/40.\par     "The moment the rupee went past 45.50 we saw state-run\par banks coming in to sell (dollars) and that has brought it back\par a little but sentiment still remains weak with some more\par month-end demand seen emerging," a private bank dealer said.\par     "Unless we see a decisive break past 45.40, the rupee will\par likely end weaker today as well, as dollar supplies remain\par thin."\par     Dollar demand from importers, including oil firms, who\par traditionally step up purchases towards the end of the month\par has been weighing on the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  The rupee has lost\par nearly one-fifth of a percent in the past three sessions.\par     Rupee discounts on forward dollars also compressed as the\par increased availability of cash dollars -- partly the result of\par dollar-selling by state-run banks -- combined with increased\par hedging by importers to make forwards dearer.\par     The annualised discount on the six-month dollar narrowed to\par 0.44 percent, from its previous close of 0.54 percent. Forward\par dollars have 