\par\par \b \uc2 Indian rupee steady early, eyes govt formation.  \b0\par\par\uc2 395 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Mai 2004\par 06:33\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     BOMBAY, May 21 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee was steady on\par Friday morning, with traders wary over the pace of reforms by\par the new Congress-led government and as allies' bickering over\par key posts added to political uncertainty.\par     At 9:30 a.m. (0400 GMT), the rupee          was quoted at\par 45.3450/3600 per dollar, barely changed from the previous close\par of 45.3300/3500.\par     It had eased 0.25 percent on Thursday, as cheer over the\par nomination of the pioneer of India's economic reforms, Manmohan\par Singh, to the post of prime minister had given way to wariness\par over the fate of some key economic reforms.\par     Traders said the rupee could take its cue from the stock\par market, where the mood is expected to be hurt by reports the\par coalition had not yet finalised names for key ministerial\par berths.\par     Local newspapers reported on Friday that Laloo Prasad\par Yadav, a key ally, had left Delhi in a huff after his party was\par offered the agriculture and railways ministries.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  He was\par reported to have asked for the ministry of internal affairs and\par the post of deputy prime minister.\par  \par     "I guess the noises from the political front will outweigh\par all other market moving factors," said a foreign bank dealer.\par     "So it makes sense keeping a tab on the events unfolding in\par New Delhi."\par     The rupee had fallen to an eight-month low of 46.01 per\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 dollar on Monday, on fears that foreign funds would pare  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 positions in India amid expectations that leftists - key  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 supporters of the incoming Congress-led government - would  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 block or slow economic reforms, particularly privatisation.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But it rebounded on increased \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  support and  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 after volatile stocks steadied. The Reserve Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 India\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  often  \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervenes\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  through state-run banks to curb excessive swings and  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 volatility in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Foreign funds have now sold a net of nearly $700 million of  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Indian shares in 12 sessions, well over a sixth of what they  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 had invested this year before political worries set in.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  -------------------------------------------------------------\par                                MARKET SNAPSHOT\par                                     9:30 a.m.  Thursday's close\par     Bombay Sensitive Index (pts)     to open           4,932.11\par     Indian rupee (/$)             45.3450/3600     45.3300/3500\par     7.37 percent 2014 bond (%)          5.1909           5.1909\par     Call money (%)                   4.25/4.50\par 4.00/4.25\par     6-mth fwd annualised premium\par      (discount) (%)                   to open            (0.66)\par -------------------------------------------------------------.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040521e05l000ki\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
