\par\par \b \uc2 Indian rupee seen firmer, eyes reformist PM.  \b0\par\par\uc2 385 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Mai 2004\par 05:24\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     BOMBAY, May 20 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee is expected to\par rise for the fourth successive day on Thursday, with sentiment\par upbeat as Manmohan Singh, the architect of reforms in Asia's\par third-largest economy, is set to be sworn in as prime minister.\par     Traders expect Singh to push for more economic reforms\par which could attract foreign capital inflows and push up the\par rupee.\par     Singh, a former finance minister, was chosen to lead the\par world's largest democracy after Congress leader Sonia Gandhi's\par stunning decision on Tuesday to walk away from the top job.\par     Sentiment will also be boosted by the recent rebound in the\par stock market. The benchmark Bombay share index  closed\par 2.65 percent higher, up for the second straight day on\par Wednesday.\par     Om Tuesday, the Bombay index saw its biggest daily gain\par since 1992, following the previous session's worst-ever\par intra-day points fall on fears that the new government may\par stall reforms.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     The rupee          ended Wednesday at 45.2250/2350 per\par dollar, more than half a percent higher than Tuesday's close of\par 45.4750/4850.\par  It had fallen to an eight-month low of 46.01 per dollar at the\par start of the week, on fears that foreign funds would pare\par positions in India amid expectations that leftists -- key\par supporters of the incoming Congress-led government -- would\par block or slow economic reforms, particularly privatisation.\par     But it rebounded on increased \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  support and\par after volatile stocks steadied. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 India\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  often\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervenes\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  through state-run banks to curb excessive swings and\par volatility in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market.\par     But while sentiment for the rupee was turning favourable,\par traders said they would keep an eye on foreign funds, who sold\par stocks recently amid all the political uncertainty.\par     Foreign funds have invested $3.8 billion this year, but\par sold nearly $500 million last week, dampening sentiment for the\par rupee. They have sold an about $80 million so far this week.\par  \par -------------------------------------------------------------\par                                MARKET SNAPSHOT\par                              Wednesday's close   Tuesday's\par close\par     Bombay Sensitive Index (pts)      5,006.10         4,877.02\par     Indian rupee (/$)             45.2250/2350     45.4750/4850\par     7.37 percent 2014 bond (%)          5.1819           5.1835\par     Call money (%)                   4.25/4.50        4.25/4.50\par     6-mth fwd annualised premium\par      (discount) (%)                     (0.57)           (0.17)\par -------------------------------------------------------------.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040520e05k000hf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
