\par\par \b \uc2 Indian rupee ends sharply higher, mood upbeat.  \b0\par\par\uc2 501 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Mai 2004\par 13:20\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     BOMBAY, May 19 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee rose for the\par third straight day to end sharply higher on Wednesday, with the\par stock market and rupee sentiment boosted by hopes a pro-reform\par prime minister, Manmohan Singh, would be sworn in.\par     The rupee          ended at 45.2250/2350 per dollar, more\par than half a percent higher than Tuesday's close of\par 45.4750/4850.\par  \par     "There was dollar buying by some state-run oil companies,"\par a dealer at a private sector bank said.\par     "But \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  support at lower levels helped the rupee\par to end firmer. Sentiment has also improved considerably on the\par back of the rebounding stock market."\par     India's benchmark 30-share Bombay Stock Exchange index\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 rose 2.65 percent to cross the key 5,000-level as  \f28 \par \par \uc2 investors were encouraged by firmer global markets and hopes  \par \par \uc2 that Singh will carry forward economic reforms.  \par \par \uc2 Singh, a former finance minister and architect of India's  \par \par \uc2 economic modernisation plan, was the front-runner to lead the  \par \par \uc2 world's largest democracy after Congress chief Sonia Gandhi's  \par \par \uc2 stunning decision on Tuesday to walk away from the top job.  \par \par \uc2 The rupee had fallen to an eight-month low of 46.01 per dollar  \par \par \uc2 at the start of the week, on fears that foreign funds would  \par \par \uc2 pare positions in India amid expectations that leftists - key  \par \par \uc2 supporters of the incoming Congress-led government - would  \par \par \uc2 block or slow economic reforms, particularly privatisation.  \par \par \uc2 But the local unit has rebounded on increased \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 support and after the Bombay share index registered its  \par \par \uc2 second-biggest points gain on Tuesday. Bombay shares suffered  \par \par \uc2 their biggest ever intra-day fall on Monday.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  often \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  through state-run  \par \par \uc2 banks to curb excessive swings and volatility in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 market, as record \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves of $119 billion  \par \par \uc2 give it enough ammunition.  \par \par \uc2 But while sentiment for the rupee is turning favourable,  \par \par \uc2 traders said they would keep an eye on foreign funds who sold  \par \par \uc2 stocks recently for cues amid all the political uncertainty.  \par \par \uc2 Recent comments by the communists about future  \par \par \uc2 privatisation of state-owned assets have triggered selling by  \par \par \uc2 foreign investors, whose record portfolio investments of $7.7  \par \par \uc2 billion in 2003 boosted the rupee by five percent over the  \par \par \uc2 year.  \par \par \uc2 Foreign funds have invested $3.8 billion this year, but  \par \par \uc2 sold nearly $500 million last week, dampening sentiment for the  \par \par \uc2 rupee.  \par \par \uc2 Discounts on the forward dollar widened as exporters  \par \par \uc2 conducted buy-sell swaps amid growing hopes that the rupee  \par \par \uc2 would make more headway in the next few days.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     The six-month forward ended at a discount of 0.57 percent,\par compared with the previous close of 0.17 percent.\par -------------------------------------------------------------\par                                MARKET SNAPSHOT\par                              Wednesday's close   Tuesday's\par close\par     Bombay Sensitive Index (pts)      5,006.10         4,877.02\par     Indian rupee (/$)             45.2250/2350     45.4750/4850\par     7.37 percent 2014 bond (%)          5.1795           5.1835\par     Call money (%)                   4.25/4.50        4.25/4.50\par     6-mth fwd annualised premium\par      (discount) (%)                     (0.57)           (0.17)\par -------------------------------------------------------------.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040519e05j001f7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
