\par\par \b \uc2 Indian rupee gains sharply, eyes stocks for cues.  \b0\par\par\uc2 485 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Mai 2004\par 06:25\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     BOMBAY, May 19 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee rose sharply on\par Wednesday, with sentiment boosted by hopes that a reformist\par prime minister, Manmohan Singh, would be sworn in, but traders\par said further gains would depend on the volatile stock market.\par     At 9:30 a.m. (0400 GMT), the rupee          was quoted at\par 45.2300/2600 per dollar, much firmer than the previous close of\par 45.4750/4850.\par  \par     "The rupee's rise in early trades is purely sentiment\par driven," said a chief trader at a private sector bank.\par     "We have to see where the stock market opens to know if\par there are more gains in store. A drop in global oil prices is\par also helping."\par     India's main stock markets open at 10:00 a.m. (0430 GMT)\par for trade and are expected to gain for a second straight day.\par     Singh, a former finance minister and architect of India's\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2  economic modernisation reforms, was the front-runner to lead   \par \par \uc2  the world's largest democracy after Congress chief Sonia   \par \par \uc2  Gandhi's stunning decision on Tuesday to walk away from the top   \par \par \uc2  job.   \par \par \uc2  The rupee had fallen to an eight-month low of 46.01 per dollar   \par \par \uc2  on Monday, on fears that foreign funds would pare positions in   \par \par \uc2  India amid expectations that leftists - key supporters of the   \par \par \uc2  incoming Congress-led government - would block or slow   \par \par \uc2  economic reforms, particularly privatisation.   \par \par \uc2  But it has bounced back on increased \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and after the Bombay share index registered its   \par \par \uc2  second-biggest points gain on Tuesday. Bombay shares suffered   \par \par \uc2  their biggest ever intra-day fall on Monday.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  often \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  through state-run   \par \par \uc2  banks to curb excessive swings and volatility in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2  market. Record \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves of around $119   \par \par \uc2  billion, allow the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  enough ammunition to check   \par \par \uc2  volatility in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.   \par \par \uc2  Traders said they would eye foreign funds who have sold   \par \par \uc2  stocks in recent sessions for cues amid political uncertainty   \par \par \uc2  and doubts about the pace of economic reforms.   \par \par \uc2  Reforms were stepped up by the outgoing National Democratic   \par \par \uc2  Alliance government and were crucial in drawing foreign   \par \par \uc2  investment into local assets.   \par \par \uc2  But recent comments by the communists about future   \par \par \uc2  privatisation of state-owned assets have triggered selling by   \par \par \uc2  foreign investors, whose record portfolio investments of $7.7   \par \par \uc2  billion in 2003 boosted the rupee by five percent over the   \par \par \uc2  year.   \par \par \uc2  Foreign funds have invested some $3.8 billion this year,   \par \par \uc2  which had helped the rupee rise by another five percent to a   \par \par \uc2  51-month closing high of 43.5450/5500 per dollar on April 7.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par -------------------------------------------------------------\par                           MARKET SNAPSHOT\par                                     9:30 a.m.    Tuesday's\par close\par     Bombay Sensitive Index (pts)    to open  