\par\par \b \uc2 Indian rupee seen steady, to eye cbank for support.  \b0\par\par\uc2 382 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Mai 2004\par 05:22\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     BOMBAY, May 19 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee is expected to\par trade steadily on Wednesday, with traders eyeing foreign funds\par who have sold stocks in recent sessions for cues, but the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is likely to curb sharp losses by selling the\par greenback.\par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 India\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  often \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervenes\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  through state-run\par banks to curb excessive swings and volatility in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par market.\par     The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  had fallen to an eight-month low of 46.01 per\par dollar on Monday, on fears that foreign funds would pare\par positions in India amid expectations that leftists -- key\par supporters of the incoming Congress-led government -- will\par block or slow economic reforms, particularly privatisation.\par     But it has bounced back on increased \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 India\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is sitting on record \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par reserves of around $119 billion, allowing it enough ammunition\par to check volatility in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     Traders said they would eye shares on the Bombay bourse,\par which bounced back on Tuesday from a miserable start to the\par week on hopes a reformist prime minister, Manmohan Singh, will\par be sworn in if Sonia Gandhi decides not to take the job.\par     Stocks had plunged as much as 17 percent on Monday, the\par worst fall in the exchange's 129 years, dogged by persistent\par worries about the pace of economic reforms.\par     Reforms were stepped up by the outgoing National Democratic\par Alliance government and were crucial in drawing foreign\par investment into local assets.\par     Recent comments by the communists about future\par privatisation of state-owned assets have triggered selling by\par foreign investors, whose record portfolio investments of $7.7\par billion in 2003 boosted the rupee by five percent over the\par year.\par     Foreign funds have invested some $3.8 billion this year,\par which had helped the rupee rise by another five percent to a\par 51-month closing high of 43.5450/5500 per dollar on April 7.\par  \par     But they sold nearly $500 million last week due to\par political uncertainty.\par -------------------------------------------------------------\par                           MARKET SNAPSHOT\par                                   Tuesday's close  Monday's\par close\par     Bombay Sensitive Index (pts)         4,877.02\par 4,505.16\par     Indian rupee (/$)                45.4750/4850\par 5.5150/5250\par     7.37 percent 2014 bond (%)             5.1835\par 5.2516\par     Call money (%)                      4.25/4.50\par 4.00/4.25\par     6-mth fwd annualised premium\par      (discount) (%)                        (0.17)\par (0.13)\par -------------------------------------------------------------.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040519e05j00091\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
