\par\par \b \uc2 WRAPUP 3-Indian stocks crash on fears left may stall reforms.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Giri Venkatesan and Braden Reddall  \par \uc2 865 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Mai 2004\par 16:24\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 BOMBAY, May 17 (Reuters) - Indian stocks plunged as much as 17 percent on Monday in their biggest ever intra-day drop in a frenzied sell-off on signs that a new government dependent on leftist support will struggle to push through economic reforms.  \par \par \uc2 The rupee and bonds fell sharply, having been boosted over the past year by record levels of foreign investment pouring into Asia's third-largest economy, though the rupee made a recovery on \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The market turmoil forced the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  to make an emergency statement, reassuring investors that it would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if necessary to make sure the banking system and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market worked smoothly.  \par \par \uc2 The comments helped drag markets off session lows, but stocks still ended down 11.1 percent on the day, at their lowest since October, with $27 billion of investor wealth wiped out.  \par \par \uc2 "There is absolute chaos and my retail clients have lost heavily," said Hemang Raja, chief executive of Bombay brokerage IL&FS Investsmart. "People are talking about foreign funds looking to pull out $1 billion to $2 billion from India."  \par \par \uc2 Foreign funds sold around $615 million worth of Indian shares in the nine sessions to Friday, or around 15 percent of what they had invested this year prior to the sell-off.  \par \par \uc2 India's communist parties, after deciding not to sign up formally to Sonia Gandhi's Congress party and its allies, due to take office in two days, called on Monday for increased farm subsidies and a shutdown of the privatisation ministry.  \par \par \uc2 "The fact that the left parties have decided not to join the government is a further blow to sentiment," Raja said.  \par \par \uc2 TRADERS PROTEST, TRADE SUSPENDED  \par \par \uc2 The Bombay Stock Exchange and National Stock Exchange suspended trade twice on Monday after their benchmark indices fell 15.5 percent and 17.5 percent respectively.  \par \par \uc2 When markets resumed trade, buying by state-backed financial institutions helped pull benchmark indices off their lows.  \par \par \uc2 The market was closed for three hours and only open for two-and-a-half, yet volume totalled a healthy 121 million shares.  \par \par \uc2 The Bombay index closed 564.71 points lower at 4,505.16, its second-biggest daily points loss after a fall of 570.42 points on April 28, 1992, at the height of a stock market scandal. But at its low, it was down 842.37 points or 16.6 percent - a record.  \par \par \uc2 Hundreds of people, including stock brokers and dealers, piled onto the streets outside the Bombay Stock Exchange in an impromptu protest against the incoming government, screaming "Sonia Gandhi Hai Hai (Down with Sonia Gandhi)."  \par \par \uc2 Indian markets have been heading down for three weeks since exit polls first indicated the pro-reform Bharatiya Janata Party-led (BJP) government may not return. Including Monday's plunge, share markets have lost some $75 billion since then.  \par \par \uc2 "This is the worst day in our lives - we've lost everything, we're dead," said Bombay broker Sandeep Sirsalewalla.  \par \par \uc2 "We want the BJP back."  \par \par \uc2 The rupee dropped as much as 0.8 percent to an eight-month intra-day low of 46.01 rupees per dollar, before recovering in afternoon trade after the Reserve Bank statement. It ended at 45.515/525, up from Friday's close of 45.60/64.  \par \par \uc2 STATE-RUN FIRMS SUFFER  \par \par \uc2 Monday's falls were led by banks and state-run firms.  \par \par \uc2 Oil and Natural Gas Corporation, India's most valuable company, fell 13 percent to 629.75 rupees while Shipping Corporation of India slid 19 percent to 69.60 rupees.  \par \par \uc2 State Bank of India fell 13 percent and Oriental Bank of Commerce plunged 24 percent.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said the sharp slide was made worse by margin calls - in which brokers have to deposit money with the exchanges to back up transactions - following heavy losses last week.  \par \par \uc2 "Hedge funds are selling at any price. The fall today has more to do with technical positions (rather than) any political worries," said Ambareesh Baliga, vice president at Karvy Stock Broking. "Stop-losses got triggered, margin calls were made."  \par \par \uc2 Political leaders appealed for calm in the markets.  \par \par \uc2 "There is no need for panic," said Pranab Mukherjee, a senior Congress leader and a frontrunner for the finance minister's job.  \par \par \uc2 But the Communist Party of India repeated on Monday last week's statement that there was no need for a ministry charged with privatising firms.  \par \par \uc2 "The left parties should not have made such statements before the formation of the new government. It has totally demoralised the market," said Sudip Kothari, a Bombay-based businessman.  \par \par \uc2 Privatisation was one of the biggest reforms of the outgoing government, helping attract more than $10 billion in foreign equity investment into India since the start of 2003. This helped boost the Bombay share index by 73 percent last year, making it Asia's best performer after Thailand.  \par \par \uc2 The inflows also helped the rupee climb more than five percent against the dollar last year, as investors flocked to a country which is among the world's fastest-growing economies. (Additional reporting by Denny Thomas, Rina Chandran and Rosemary Arackaparambil).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040517e05h000rd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
