\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS-India may have to loosen rupee grip as economy opens.   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Beverly Mathews   \par \uc2 507 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 April 2004\par 12:42\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 BOMBAY, April 20 (Reuters) - India may have to give up its fixation with a stable exchange rate as the economy opens up, so the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  can more effectively manage money, inflation and interest rates, analysts say.   \par \par \uc2 Asia's third-biggest economy continues to encourage foreign trade and investment, but the greater inflows expected will probably mean \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility because resisting further gains by the rupee will cost too much and could lead to excessive money supply and rising prices.   \par \par \uc2 "As an economy opens up and capital inflows swell, it becomes increasingly difficult to manage the exchange rate and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could lose its impact," said Ila Patnaik, senior economist at the National Council for Applied Economic Research, New Delhi.   \par \par \uc2 The rupee has been flexible and partly convertible for about a decade, but the Reserve Bank of India has policed the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's moves as the emerging economy sought a footing in the global market.   \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has sought to contain a rupee rally in the past two years caused by a weak dollar, strong exports and investment into an economy that expanded by 10.4 percent between the October-December quarters of 2002 and 2003, making it the fastest growing major economy in the world.   \par \par \uc2 Authorities have limited the appreciation to 12 percent, to around 44 per dollar from a historic low of 49.08 in mid-2002.   \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's tireless dollar shopping has generated rupee surpluses in the banking system, estimated at $17 billion, while \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves have risen $40 billion over the past year to a record $116 billion.   \par \par \uc2 "But the cost of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  is becoming too heavy," said Patnaik.   \par \par \uc2 EXCESS LIQUIDITY   \par \par \uc2 To tackle excess rupee liquidity, India has started selling up to 600 billion rupees in "stabilisation" securities over the next year.   \par \par \uc2 But these bonds pay around 4.5 percent, much more than what the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  earns by investing reserves in low-risk securities such as U.S. treasury bills. Besides, the government cannot issue bonds indiscriminately.   \par \par \uc2 "The cost of \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  (\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) is made more contentious by \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's worrying fiscal finances," said Rajeev Malik, economist with JP Morgan in Singapore.   \par \par \uc2 He estimated the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's net interest loss at around 0.4 percent of gross domestic product (GDP) in the last fiscal year to March.   \par \par \uc2 Some analysts also say the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market has evolved and does not need the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to manage day-to-day volatility, while exporters will not be devastated by a stronger rupee.   \par \par \uc2 "There could be a sharp adjustment if \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  stops but after a while, capital flows could slow as some money is coming in on expectations of an appreciation," Patnaik said.   \par \par \uc2 There may also be less need for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 Exporters, who are affected by \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves, are riding on their own competitiveness. Goods exports surged by more than 17 percent in the year to March. Services exports more than doubled in April-December, 2003 over the previous year.    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040420e04k001k7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
