\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 India FX \highlight\uc2  reserves at new high on c.bank  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2 .  \b0\par\par\uc2 541 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 April 2004\par 11:19\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     By Anirban Nag\par     BOMBAY, April 17 (Reuters) - \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 India\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par reserves soared to a record $116.06 billion in the week ended\par April 9 as capital inflows gathered pace and the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par stepped up its presence in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market, analysts said.\par     Data from the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , said India's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par reserves rose by $3.37 billion -- one of its highest weekly\par gains -- from $112.690 billion in the week ended April 2.\par     The Reserve Bank of India (RBI) said the foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par assets expressed in U.S. dollar terms included the effect of\par appreciation/depreciation of other \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  held in its\par reserves such as the euro, pound sterling and yen.\par     Analysts said the rapid reserve accumulation largely\par reflects the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's determination to stem sharp gains by\par the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  against the U.S. dollar to protect export\par competitiveness.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     The Reserve Bank of India has stepped up its dollar-buying,\par after arming itself in the new fiscal year from April 1 with\par stabilisation bonds to offset the effects of its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par     The new securities will be sold in the market to drain the\par rupees injected by the bank's dollar purchases. India plans to\par sell up to 600 billion rupees ($13.67 billion) of the bonds in\par the current financial year to March 2005.\par     The Indian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  which has been grappling with steady\par foreign capital inflows in the past two years, had almost run\par out of rupee assets to offset the inflows towards the latter\par half of March.\par     Analysts say this might have prompted the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to\par stay away from the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market during that period allowing\par the tightly policed rupee to gain about 3.6 percent to hit\par multi-year highs.\par     "The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was expected after the market\par stabilisation bonds came into the picture in the new financial\par year," said Dhananjay Sinha, economist with ICICI Bank.\par     The rupee          ended Friday at 43.8850/8950 per dollar,\par down half a percent during the week as the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  soaked\par up foreign capital inflows.\par  \par  \par     ROBUST CAPITAL INFLOWS\par     But the partially convertible Indian rupee has gained about\par four percent against the dollar since January on robust foreign\par investments in one of the world's fastest growing economies.\par     Data from the capital market showed net purchases by\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 overseas funds were at $490 million so far in April, taking  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 their total net bond and stock purchases to $4.0 billion in  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2004.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The surge in reserves suggests that the pressure of capital  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 inflows has stepped up in recent times," said Siddharth Mathur,  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 strategist at JP Morgan Chase, Bombay. "This presents an  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 interesting challenge for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  managment to the RBI."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Foreign investments are surging into the Indian economy  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 which is estimated to have grown by 8.1 percent in the financial  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 year to March 2004, and is likely to grow at more than six  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 percent in the current financial year on the back of growing  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 demand for manufactured goods and services.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Institute of International Finance said international  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 capital inflows to developing market jumped $194 billion in 2003  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 from a 10-year low of $128 billion a year ago and could rise by  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 15 percent in 2004.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040417e04h004ed\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
