\par\par \b \uc2 Indian rupee seen adding gains - all eyes on cbank.  \b0\par\par\uc2 454 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 April 2004\par 05:09\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     BOMBAY, April 2 (Reuters) - The buoyant Indian rupee is\par expected to extend gains into a tenth straight session on\par Friday on strong foreign capital inflows but traders said they\par would watch the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  closely for signs of renewed\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par     "Everyone is keen to see the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's stance on the\par first trading day of the new fiscal year," a dealer at a\par state-run bank said.\par     "While the pressure on the rupee to appreciate is quite\par strong, how much and how fast it gains will all depend on\par whether the RBI (Reserve Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 India\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ) resumes its aggressive\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  or keeps away like they have done since last\par week."\par     India's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market was shut on the first day of the\par current fiscal year on Thursday, as banks closed their annual\par accounts for the year that ended on March 31, 2004.\par     Traders expect the rupee          to climb higher from its\par nearly four-year closing peak set on Wednesday, when it ended\par with a gain of 0.9 percent at 43.6500/7000 per dollar.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     It last closed higher at 43.6775/6825 on May 9, 2000.\par     "More ECB (external commercial borrowing) inflows are due\par to come in and if the RBI stays away we should see a range of\par 43.10 to 43.40. But if the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  comes in, a correction\par to 44 is quite possible," a dealer at a private bank said.\par     The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , armed with fresh rupee assets under a new\par stabilisation scheme, is seen resuming its dollar buying but\par analysts are unsure of how aggressive it may be after India's\par Finance Minister hinted that a stronger rupee could help in\par inflation management.\par     "The end of large scale \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the RBI is\par permanent," said Barclays Capital's economist Dominique\par Dwor-Freaut in a recent research report.\par     "With a capital account open on the inflow side, the RBI\par can no longer ignore the trade-off between competitiveness and\par inflation," she said in the report, recommending clients to\par stay short on the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  against the rupee.\par     The RBI loosened its grip on the rupee last week,\par coinciding with a surge in foreign funds' remittances towards\par local share offerings.\par     The rupee has soared 3.5 percent in nine straight sessions,\par taking its total appreciation against the dollar in\par January-March 2004 to 4.4 percent.\par  \par -------------------------------------------------------------\par                              MARKET SNAPSHOT\par                               Thursday's close  Wednesday's\par close\par      Bombay Sensitive Index (pts)     5,740.85\par 5,590.60\par      Indian rupee (/$)                no deals\par 43.6500/7000\par      7.37 percent 2014 bond (%)         5.1279\par 5.1462\par      Call money (%)                   no deals\par 3.50/4.00\par      6-mth fwd annualised premium (%) no deals\par 0.88\par -------------------------------------------------------------.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040402e042000jo\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex