\par\par \b \uc2 Indian Rupee At INR43.42/Dlr; Highest Since Dec 3, 1999  \b0\par\par\uc2 436 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 08:25\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI (Dow Jones)--The Indian rupee breached several key resistance levels Wednesday as it rallied to a 52-month high against the dollar on continued capital inflows and a growing conviction that the government favors a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The local unit took out psychological barriers at INR44.00, INR43.75 and INR43.50 against the dollar to touch an intraday high of INR43.40, last tested on Dec. 1, 1999. The rupee closed at INR44.07 Monday. The \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market was shut Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 The rupee's rise was driven mainly by strong and persistent capital inflows following a string of privatization sales held recently by the government. The government sold part of its holdings in six state-run companies earlier this month to raise a total $3.4 billion.  \par \par \uc2 But the local unit gained additional upward momentum Wednesday following comments by a top government official Tuesday, which suggested the government is favoring a strong local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as this helps in bringing down inflation.  \par \par \uc2 Finance Minister Jaswant Singh said Tuesday that his Bharatiya Janata Party, the leader of India's ruling coalition, "remains committed to a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate" as "a strong rupee has relationships with inflation management and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  flows."  \par \par \uc2 The rupee has also benefited from notably weak resistance to its rise from state-owned banks, which have bought dollars almost routinely in recent months on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to protect exports.  \par \par \uc2 Last Friday, RBI Governor Y.V. Reddy said the rupee's volatility against the dollar was still less than other international \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fluctuations. He also stressed the rise was entirely market-driven - a comment taken by many in the market to mean the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is comfortable with a stronger rupee.  \par \par \uc2 "The Reserve Bank Of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  has scaled down its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , possibly because the privatization-related capital inflows aren't speculative investments," says a local analyst. "Also, these inflows aren't debt-creating."  \par \par \uc2 The rupee has gained 8.6% against the dollar in the past 12 months on capital inflows and general dollar weakness.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  watchers expect the rupee to continue its uptrend in coming weeks, albeit at a slower pace, with some forecasting it to test INR42.50 in the next one year.  \par \par \uc2 "Apart from higher inflows arising due to foreign subscriptions in recent privatizations, we believe a key reason for the recent rise is the (RBI's) limited ammunition to sterilize them," says a Citigroup report.  \par \par \uc2 -By Anil Varma, Dow Jones Newswires; 91 22 22884212; anil.varma@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Ryan Woo [ 31-03-04 0725GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040331e03v0008z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
