\par\par \b \uc2 Bond plan means India rupee surge may be its last.   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Anirban Nag   \par \uc2 645 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 13:50\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 BOMBAY, March 26 (Reuters) - This week's near 1.5 percent rise of the rupee against the dollar is unlikely to be repeated because India's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is about to get new ammunition to help it smooth \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fluctuations, analysts said.   \par \par \uc2 The rupee's strengthening represented its highest weekly gain in six years, and took it to a 46-month closing high of 44.47/48 on Friday.   \par \par \uc2 It followed a firming of a mere 0.8 percent in the 11 weeks to March 18.   \par \par \uc2 The Indian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stayed away from \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  this week, but will probably be back in the fiscal year that begins on April 1, armed with sizeable funds for its new market stabilisation scheme.   \par \par \uc2 "They will be able to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  more aggressively once they have the stabilisation (scheme) in their armoury," said Dhananjay Sinha, economist at ICICI Bank.   \par \par \uc2 On Thursday, India said it will issue stabilisation bonds or bills for 355 billion rupees ($7.9 billion) in the first quarter of the new fiscal year, giving the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  more ammunition to offset the rupee's rise on foreign capital inflows.   \par \par \uc2 Capital inflows into India, one of the main drivers of the rupee's gains, were a record $7.7 billion in 2003 and lifted the rupee 5.2 percent last year. Foreign funds have already invested more than $2 billion in the rapidly expanding economy this year.   \par \par \uc2 India's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , like most of its regional counterparts, had been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market for the past two years to curb sharp gains by the rupee.   \par \par \uc2 In the process, it has built up record \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves of $110 billion. It has also flooded domestic money markets with excess rupees, which it must mop up by selling bonds, a process called sterilisation.   \par \par \uc2 "At a time when \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  inflows into the country are set to rise in the near term, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  needed a huge chest as the current instruments available for sterilisation are not enough," said Shubhada Rao, an economist in the Bank of Baroda's risk management group.   \par \par \uc2 That shortage of rupee assets may have kept the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  from buying dollars for rupees in recent days, analysts said.   \par \par \uc2 FLOODED WITH RUPEES   \par \par \uc2 The Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's (RBI) \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  have flooded the domestic money market, with excess cash estimated at around 550-600 billion rupees.   \par \par \uc2 The RBI has been draining that excessive liquidity by conducting daily repo auctions and by selling government bonds from its own investment portfolio in the market.   \par \par \uc2 But analysts estimate the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  holds only about 800-900 billion rupees in securities, down from 1.5 trillion rupees on March, 2001 as it has been selling bonds to absorb excess funds from the money market.   \par \par \uc2 Nearly 500 billion rupees worth of bonds are used to drain funds on a daily basis through the repurchase auctions, leaving the RBI few securities to absorb rupees for longer durations.   \par \par \uc2 There should be no such shortage of assets in the coming fiscal year. Authorities have set aside up to 600 billion rupees for the stabilisation scheme in the financial year to March 2005.   \par \par \uc2 That should offer the RBI some relief, because the factors behind the rupee's gains have not changed. Traders said the recent gains were due to foreign funds chasing $3 billion of asset sales by state-run companies in February and March.   \par \par \uc2 With India expected to grow by over eight percent in the year to March, analysts expect foreign capital will continue to flow into Asia's third-largest economy.   \par \par \uc2 "We see continued strong inflows through portfolio investments, export earnings and renewed dollar weakness in global \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets," JP Morgan said in a report on Friday.   \par \par \uc2 "The rupee will continue to face appreciation pressure in coming months."    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040326e03q001su\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
