\par\par \b \uc2 India rupee ends firm for 6th day, but inflows ebb.  \b0\par\par\uc2 449 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 12:19\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     BOMBAY, March 25 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee rose for the\par sixth straight session on Thursday to hit a new 44-month\par closing high but recent heavy foreign fund inflows were showing\par signs of tapering, dealers said.\par     Traders said dollar purchases by some companies checked the\par rupee's gains on Thursday after it rose 1.1 percent against the\par dollar in the past five sessions.\par     The rupee          ended at 44.7400/7450 per dollar,\par marginally higher than Wednesday's close of 44.7450/7550 which\par was also its previous 44-month closing high.\par     It last ended firmer on July 17, 2000 at 44.7125/7175.\par     The rupee, which analysts say is fairly valued on a\par trade-weighted basis, has appreciated by nearly two percent so\par far in 2004 after rising by 5.2 percent last year on strong\par trade and foreign fund inflows.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     Overseas funds have chased a slew of recent public share\par issues totalling about $3 billion, which included a record\par offering of over $2 billion by India's most valuable firm,\par state-run explorer Oil and Natural Gas Corp Ltd (ONGC)\par  (ONGC.BO).\par     Thursday was the last day for these funds to remit the\par amounts they had bid at the ONGC offering. Allotments begin\par next week.\par     Traders said another factor helping the rupee's rally in\par recent sessions was the relatively limited \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  from\par the Reserve Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 India\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  (RBI), which had earlier purchased\par dollars heavily to brake the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's rise.\par     RBI Governor Y.V. Reddy said during the session the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central\par bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was aimed mainly at curbing market\par volatility, belying market speculation its recent actions were\par aimed at containing inflation in a rapidly-expanding economy.\par     "That type of linkage (between inflationary concerns and\par the exchange rate) should not be seen," Reddy said. "The\par (exchange rate) is based on demand and supply and all we try to\par do is to ensure that there is not much volatility."\par  \par     India's economy, Asia's third-largest, is forecast to grow\par by more than eight percent in 2003/04 (April-March).\par     Rupee premiums on the forward dollar eased on a bullish\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 outlook for the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , but traders expect some hedging  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 by importers soon. The six-month forward ended at an annualised  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0.67 percent, down from the previous close of 0.73 percent.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par -------------------------------------------------------------\par                              MARKET SNAPSHOT\par                                  Thursday close Wednesday's\par close\par      Bombay Sensitive Index (pts)  5,414.44           5,395.28\par      Indian rupee (/$)            44.7400/7450    44.7450/7550\par      7.37 percent 2014 bond (%)     5.1675              5.1738\par      Call money (%)                 4.25/4.50        4.00/4.25\par      6-mth fwd annualised premium (%) 0.67                0.73\par -------------------------------------------------------------.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040325e03p0028z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
