\par\par \b \uc2 Indian rupee seen firming sixth day, eyes c.bank.  \b0\par\par\uc2 389 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 04:10\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     BOMBAY, March 25 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee is seen\par stretching its rally to a sixth straight session on Thursday,\par with the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  appearing to have loosened its grip over\par the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  amid strong foreign fund inflows, dealers said.\par     "We should see more gains for the rupee since another 200\par to 300 million dollars of foreign fund remittances are expected\par to come in towards the IPO (initial public offering) bids," a\par dealer at a state-run bank said. "The range should be 44.68 to\par 44.78."\par     The rupee          appreciated by 0.28 percent on\par Wednesday, setting a 44-month closing high of 44.7450/7550 per\par dollar. It last closed higher at 44.7400/7500 on July 24, 2000.\par     Overseas funds had bid strongly during a slew of recent\par public share issues totalling about $3 billion, which included\par a record offering of over $2 billion by India's most valuable\par firm, state-run explorer Oil and Natural Gas Corp (ONGC)\par  (ONGC.BO).\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     Thursday is the last day for these funds to remit the\par amounts they had bid at the ONGC offering. Allotments begin\par next week.\par     Traders said the Reserve Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 India\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's minimal\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to contain rupee appreciation in the past couple\par of sessions had been a key factor aiding the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's rally.\par  \par     The rupee has appreciated by 1.1 percent in just four\par sessions, after rising by 0.8 percent in 11 weeks to March 18.\par     Analysts say the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may have backed away from its\par hitherto tight policing of the rupee in an attempt to contain\par inflationary pressures in the rapidly expanding economy.\par     Traders also pointed to the latest data from the capital\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 market regulator, which showed foreign funds' net purchases of  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 local shares on Tuesday alone totalled $116 million - a clear  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 indicator of the volume of portfolio investments pouring in.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     Foreign funds' total investments into Indian assets so far\par in 2004 are now well over the $2.0 billion mark.\par -------------------------------------------------------------\par                              MARKET SNAPSHOT\par                              Wednesday's close     Previous\par close\par      Bombay Sensitive Index (pts)     5,395.28\par 5,400.99\par      Indian rupee (/$)            44.7450/7550\par 44.8750/8800\par      7.37 percent 2014 bond (%)         5.1738\par 5.1800\par      Call money (%)                  4.00/4.25\par 4.00/4.25\par      6-mth fwd annualised premium (%)     0.73\par 0.55\par -------------------------------------------------------------.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040325e03p000gq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
