\par\par \b \uc2 India Rupee Closes Higher On Capital Inflows;Bonds Up  \b0\par\par\uc2 603 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 14:25\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI (Dow Jones)--The Indian rupee extended its rise against the dollar to a fresh 44-month high Wednesday, on continuing strong capital inflows following recent privatizations.  \par \par \uc2 The local unit ended at INR44.75 against the dollar Wednesday, a level last tested July 24, 2000. The rupee closed at INR44.87 Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 "The foreign investments in the recent privatizations have literally flooded the banking system with dollars," a dealer at a local bank said. The government sold part of its holdings in six state-run companies earlier this month to raise a total $3.4 billion.  \par \par \uc2 The rupee's gains were underscored by unusually weak dollar demand from state-owned banks, which have been routinely buying dollars on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent months to protect exports.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts now say the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  has stepped down its market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , since the privatization-related capital inflows aren't speculative investments or debt-creating.  \par \par \uc2 The rupee is expected to continue rising in coming weeks with some analysts forecasting it will test INR43.80 by the end of September. The rupee has gained 5.7% against the dollar in the past 12 months on capital inflows and general dollar weakness.  \par \par \uc2 In the forward market, dollar premiums ended higher Wednesday as sellers of the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the spot market bought it back in the forward segment to square positions. The annualized premium over the spot rate on the one-year forward contract ended at 0.63%, up from 0.58% at the previous close.  \par \par \uc2 Mifor-based interest rate swaps also rose Wednesday, in line with forward-dollar premiums, with the two-year swap rising to 2.51%-2.56% from 2.46%-2.50% at the previous close. Mifor is the Mumbai interbank forward offered rate. It is derived from dollar forward rates.  \par \par \uc2 Government bond prices ended higher Wednesday on liquidity-driven buying. Record high bids at the RBI's daily repurchase agreement auction indicated a rise in money market liquidity, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  accepted bids totaling INR555 billion at its repo Wednesday, up from INR531 billion Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 Bond yields edged down across the board, with the 10-year bond yield ending at 5.17%, down from 5.18% at the previous close.  \par \par \uc2 Bond prices are expected to move in a narrow range around current levels in the coming days, as traders maintain near-square positions ahead of the March 31 financial year end.  \par \par \uc2 The overnight interbank call money rate ended steady at 4.00%-4.30% Wednesday, compared with 4.00%-4.25% at the previous close. [ 24-03-04 1325GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Rupee Vs Dollar Close \par Wednesday    Previous \par INR44.7500   INR44.8700 \par  \par Forward Dollar Premiums \par May                    September              February \par INR0.090 vs INR0.065   INR0.155 vs INR0.130   INR0.270 vs INR0.245 \par  \par Daily Repurchase Auction Amount \par Wednesday         Previous \par INR554.75 Bln     INR530.90 Bln \par  \par Repurchase Auction Cutoff \par Wednesday      Previous \par 4.5%             4.5% \par  \par Call Rate \par Wednesday      Previous Close \par 4.00%-4.30%      4.00%-4.25% \par  \par Interest Rate Swaps  Current Price   Previous Price \par  2-Year Mifor swap   2.51%-2.56%     2.46%-2.50% \par  5-Year OIS          4.81%-4.84%     4.82%-4.83% \par  \par Benchmark Bond Prices \par Security   Matur