\par\par \b \uc2 Indian Rupee At 45.00/Dlr; Highest Since July 31, 2000  \b0\par\par\uc2 501 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 06:29\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI (Dow Jones)--The Indian rupee broke yet another psychological barrier against the dollar Tuesday, hitting a 44-month high on strong capital fund inflows following a string of privatization deals between the government and foreign investors, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 The local unit broke through the much-watched level of INR45.000 to the dollar to touch an intraday high of INR44.9650, last tested on July 31, 2000. The rupee closed at INR45.100 Monday.  \par \par \uc2 India's relatively high interest rates and rallying stock markets have attracted foreign capital for over a year now, but such inflows have strengthened significantly since last week due to foreign buying of the government's stake sales.  \par \par \uc2 The Indian government sold part of its holdings in six state-run companies earlier this month to raise a total $3.4 billion. A significant part of the sale proceeds are believed to have come from foreign investors. [ 23-03-04 0558GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 The rupee is also benefiting from notably weak resistance to its rise from state-owned banks, which have bought dollars almost routinely in recent months on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to protect exports.  \par \par \uc2 "The Reserve Bank Of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  has scaled down its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , possibly because the privatization-related capital inflows aren't speculative investments; they are real inflows," says Siddharth Mathur, head of research at JP Morgan in Mumbai. "Also, these inflows aren't debt-creating."  \par \par \uc2 The rupee has gained 5.7% against the dollar in the past 12 months on capital inflows and general dollar weakness.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  watchers expect the rupee to continue its uptrend in coming weeks, albeit at a slower pace, with some forecasting it to test INR43.80 by end-September.  \par \par \uc2 "As long as the state-run banks don't step in to support it, the dollar/rupee will continue to fall," said Rohan Lasrado, a \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at HDFC Bank in Mumbai.  \par \par \uc2 However, Indian exporters are alarmed by the rupee's relentless rise, and have voiced worries in recent months that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements could slow growth and erode the country's trade earnings.  \par \par \uc2 While exports may just about meet the government's target of a 12% growth in the current fiscal year ending March 31, a strengthening rupee erodes the chances of higher growth, said Subash Mittal, vice president of the Federation of Indian Export Organizations, an exporters' umbrella body.  \par \par \uc2 "The rupee's strength has already been hurting exports, now it will further damp growth in the sector," Mittal said. "We could have achieved 20% export growth this fiscal year if the rupee hadn't appreciated so much."  \par \par \uc2 Still, despite repeated calls by trade organizations to stem the rupee's rise, the RBI may not prune the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's gains as long as it is led by non-speculative capital flows.  \par \par \uc2 -By Anil Varma, Dow Jones Newswires; 91 22 22884212; anil.varma@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Ryan Woo and Dawn Lye [ 23-03-04 0647GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040323e03n0005q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
