\par\par \b \uc2 Indian Rupee Extends Gains To 44-Mo High Vs Dlr; Bonds Dn  \b0\par\par\uc2 673 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 14:23\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI (Dow Jones)--The Indian rupee rallied to a 44-month high Tuesday, breaching key resistance of INR45 to the dollar on strong capital inflows following a string of privatizations.  \par \par \uc2 The local unit ended at INR44.87 Tuesday, a level last tested on July 31, 2000. The rupee closed at INR45.100 Monday.  \par \par \uc2 "Capital inflows have strengthened significantly since last week due to foreign investment in the government's stake sales," said a dealer at a local bank. "And we expect more to follow."  \par \par \uc2 The Indian government sold part of its holdings in six state-run companies earlier this month to raise a total $3.4 billion.  \par \par \uc2 The rupee also benefited from unusually weak resistance to its rise from state-owned banks, which have been buying dollars on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  almost routinely in recent months in order to protect exports.  \par \par \uc2 "The Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  has scaled down its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , possibly because the privatization-related capital inflows aren't speculative investments; they are real inflows," said Siddharth Mathur, head of research at JP Morgan in Mumbai. "Also, these inflows aren't debt-creating."  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  watchers expect the rupee to continue its uptrend in coming weeks with some forecasting it will test INR43.80 by end-September. The rupee has gained 5.7% against the dollar in the past 12 months on capital inflows and general dollar weakness.  \par \par \uc2 "As long as the state-run banks don't step in to support it, dollar/rupee will continue to fall," said Rohan Lasrado, a \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at HDFC Bank in Mumbai.  \par \par \uc2 In the forward market, dollar premiums ended slightly lower Tuesday, tracking the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fall in the spot market. The annualized premium over the spot rate on the one-year forward contract ended at 0.58%, down from 0.60% at the previous close.  \par \par \uc2 Mifor-based interest rate swaps also softened Tuesday, in line with forward-dollar premiums, with the two-year swap easing to 2.46%-2.50% from 2.50%-2.54% at the previous close. Mifor is the Mumbai interbank forward offered rate. It is derived from dollar forward rates.  \par \par \uc2 Government bond prices ended lower Tuesday as profit-taking continued following last week's gains.  \par \par \uc2 Bond yields rose across the board, with the 10-year bond yield ending at 5.18%, up from 5.16% at the previous close.  \par \par \uc2 Bond prices had gained significantly late last week after data showed lower-than-expected inflation. Annual inflation fell to 4.91% in the week ended March 6, well below the expected 5.25%, and 5.32% a week earlier.  \par \par \uc2 With inflation expected to moderate further and liquidity remaining ample, bond prices could gain slightly in the coming days. However, any significant rise in prices will probably be partially offset by profit-taking ahead of the March 31 fiscal year-end.  \par \par \uc2 The overnight interbank call money rate ended lower at 4.00%-4.25% Tuesday, compared with 4.30%-4.40% at the previous close. [ 23-03-04 1323GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Rupee Vs Dollar Close \par Tuesday      Previous \par INR44.8700   INR45.1000 \par  \par Forward Dollar Premiums \par May                     September              February \par INR0.0650 vs INR0.0700  INR0.130 vs INR0.135   INR0.245 vs INR0.255 \par  \par Daily Repurchase Auction Amount \par Tuesday           Previous \par INR530.90