\par\par \b \uc2 WEEKAHEAD-Indian bonds seen rising on liquidity glut.   \b0\par\par\uc2 553 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 05:52\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     BOMBAY, March 21 (Reuters) - Indian federal bonds are\par expected to extend their rise into a third straight week,\par supported by huge inflows from bonds that are maturing and on\par expectations of a dip in inflation, analysts said.\par     But chances that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to absorb\par some of the excess funds by selling bonds from its own\par portfolio will keep gains muted, they added.\par     Proceeds from two bonds maturing during the week total\par nearly 162 billion rupees ($3.6 billion) and would add to the\par surplus cash pile of about 400 billion to 450 billion rupees\par that banks are already sitting on currently.\par  "There should be some aggressive open-market sale of bonds by\par the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," said Dhananjay Sinha, economist at ICICI\par Bank. "But that will not impact sentiment much and we could see\par the 10-year bond yield easing to 5.10 percent."\par     The benchmark 10-year bond             eased to 5.1583\par percent on Saturday, down from 5.1836 percent in the previous\par week.\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     India's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  deputy governor, Rakesh Mohan, told\par reporters in Dubai on Saturday that he had not ruled out the\par use of open market sales to mop up surplus cash from the\par system.\par     Mohan also reiterated the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would continue to\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market to avoid volatility. Traders\par said that was likely to lead to the infusion of more rupee\par funds into the system, supporting support bond prices.\par     The tightly policed rupee has appreciated nearly one\par percent against the dollar this year, and could have gained\par more but for the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par     The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has seen \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 India\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves\par rise to a record $109.6 billion as the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has\par absorbed robust foreign investment inflows and export receipts.\par     LOWER INFLATION\par     Bond market sentiment will also be supported by a continued\par fall in India's 12-month inflation rate, which analysts expect\par it to ease to around 4.5 percent by the March 31 end of\par financial year.\par     Inflation, as measured by wholesale price index, has\par recently begun showing a decline after hovering around six\par percent for most of 2004. Data released on Friday showed\par inflation in the year to March 6 had fallen to 4.91 percent,\par way below the 6.21 percent peak seen in January.\par     The rate has fallen to below five percent for the first\par time in three months and is expected fall further due to lower\par food prices and a higher base effect, analysts said.\par     "We could see inflation finish the financial year at around\par 4.5 percent," said Indranil Pan, chief economist at Kotak\par Mahindra Bank. "This along with the ample liquidity in the\par system will help maintain the momentum in the bond market."\par      Mohan said on Saturday the trend was in line with the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's January forecast, in which it estimated\par inflation to end the financial year at 4.0 to 4.5 percent.\par     Traders said they would await the government's borrowing\par calendar for the first half of 2004/05 (April-March), which is\par likely to be announced on March 25, for cues.\par     India relies heavily on its domestic debt market and public\par savings of around 26 percent of gross domestic product (GDP) to\par bridge the stubbornly high fiscal deficit.\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040321e03l002mh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
