\par\par \b \uc2 Indian Bonds Recoup Losses, End Mostly Flat; Rupee Steady  \b0\par\par\uc2 525 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 14:44\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI (Dow Jones)--Indian government bond prices ended mostly steady Thursday, after recovering from earlier lows on liquidity-driven buying.  \par \par \uc2 Earlier in the session, bonds slipped as traders took profits after Wednesday's sharp gains, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 The 10-year bond yield ended flat at 5.17%.  \par \par \uc2 "The market doesn't have a clear direction," a dealer at a local bank said. "Liquidity is plentiful, tomorrow's data should show lower inflation: nothing is negative for now. But nobody wants to risk taking big positions on fears the (Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to tighten liquidity."  \par \par \uc2 Analysts polled by Dow Jones Newswires forecast Friday's weekly data to show inflation fell to 5.25% in the week ended March 6, from 5.32% a week earlier.  \par \par \uc2 Moderating inflation and ample liquidity could boost bond prices further in the coming days, but traders said the gains will probably be partially offset by profit-taking ahead of the March 31 fiscal year-end.  \par \par \uc2 The overnight interbank call money rate ended at 4.25%-4.40% Thursday, virtually unchanged from 4.30%-4.40% at the previous close, amid ample supply of funds.  \par \par \uc2 The rupee ended nearly flat against the dollar Thursday as state-run banks absorbed inflows of the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from exporters and foreign portfolio investors.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar closed at INR45.2425, little changed from INR45.2450 at the previous close.  \par \par \uc2 Traders say the rupee is likely to strengthen in the coming months on capital inflows and general dollar weakness, although the pace of its rise may depend on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 In the forward market, dollar premiums edged up on paying by state-owned banks. The annualized premium over the spot rate on the one-year forward contract ended at 0.52%, up from 0.51% at the previous close.  \par \par \uc2 Mifor-based interest rate swaps ended slightly higher, in line with dollar forward premiums. The two-year swap ended at 2.48%-2.52%, compared with 2.45%-2.50% at Wednesday's close. Mifor is the Mumbai interbank forward offered rate. It is derived from dollar forward rates. [ 18-03-04 1344GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Rupee Vs Dollar Close \par Thursday     Previous \par INR45.2425   INR45.2450 \par  \par Forward Dollar Premiums \par May                     September              February \par INR0.0525 vs INR0.0500  INR0.115 vs INR0.110   INR0.235 vs INR0.230 \par  \par Daily Repurchase Auction Amount \par Thursday          Previous \par INR423.95 Bln     INR484.15 Bln \par  \par Repurchase Auction Cutoff \par Thursday       Previous \par 4.5%             4.5% \par  \par Call Rate \par Thursday       Previous Close \par 4.25%-4.40%      4.30%-4.40% \par  \par Interest Rate Swaps  Current Price   Previous Price \par  2-Year Mifor swap   2.48%-2.52%     2.45%-2.50% \par  5-Year OIS          4.84%-4.88%     4.85%-4.87% \par  \par Benchmark Bond Prices \par Security   Maturity   Current Price   Previous Price \par  7.40% GOI   2012        115.00          115.02 \par  7.37% GOI   2014        117.07          117.07 \par  8.07% GOI   2017        123.70          123.65 \par  6.05% GOI   2019        103.65          103.66 \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Anil Varma, Dow Jones Newswires; 91 22 2288 4212; anil.varma@dowjones.com [ 18-03-04 1344GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 1164 1847 62506  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040318e03i000om\par }\p