\par\par \b \uc2 Indian rupee to inch up on IPO inflows, cbank eyed.  \b0\par\par\uc2 379 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 04:12\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     BOMBAY, March 15 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee is expected\par to gain early on Monday on the back of fresh inflows of foreign\par capital but dealers said they would remain wary of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to check the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's appreciation.\par     "We should see more inflows on account of the IPO (initial\par public offering) bids and that should help the rupee to firm.\par But the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will still hold the key to the extent of\par rise," said Surendra Rosha, head of treasury sales at HSBC.\par     A slew of public issues including several government stake\par sales have hit the market since the last week of February and\par capital inflows are expected to continue this week from\par overseas investors who had successfully bid at these share\par offerings.\par     Traders said the rupee was likely to trade in a 45.23 to\par 45.26 per dollar range, with the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's stance being a\par key factor in determining the extent of movement.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     "The first thing to watch will be whether they (state-run\par banks that buy on behalf of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ) let go of the\par 45.25 level and after that how much further they permit the\par rupee to move," a private bank dealer said.\par  \par     "If they stay away, a test of 45.20 is possible this week,"\par he added.\par     The rupee          ended last week with a gain of nearly\par one-tenth of a percent to close at 45.2550/2600 per dollar.\par     It struck a 43-month closing high of 45.2250/2300 last\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Wednesday, but surrendered its gains the following day on\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par sustained dollar buying by state-run banks amid a bounce-back\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par by the dollar overseas.\par   \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the Reserve Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 India\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , which analysts\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par say is keen to check the rupee's rise to preserve the\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par competitiveness of local exports, have boosted the country's\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     The rupee has appreciated nearly 0.8 percent against the\par dollar so far this year, following a 5.2 percent rise in 2003.\par -------------------------------------------------------------\par                                       MARKET SNAPSHOT\par                                               Previous close\par     Bombay Sensitive Index (pts)                    5,700.40\par     Indian rupee (/$)                           45.2550/2600\par     7.37 percent 2014 bond (%)                        5.1836\par     Call money (%)                                 4.25/4.50\par     6-mth fwd annualised premium (%)                    0.35\par -------------------------------------------------------------.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040315e03f0005s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
