\par\par \b \uc2 Indian rupee seen rising, test of 45.23/dlr eyed.  \b0\par\par\uc2 302 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 03:55\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 BOMBAY, March 9 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee is expected to strengthen for a third session on Tuesday and is likely to test the 45.23 per dollar mark on the back of continuing offshore capital inflows, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 But a significant appreciation beyond 45.23 is seen unlikely in the face of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  determination to cap the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's gains to protect the competitiveness of local exports.  \par \par \uc2 "Any move say towards 45.22 is likely to trigger prompt RBI (Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," a chief dealer at a North American bank said. "Our view is that they will aim to ensure that the rupee ends this fiscal within the 45.20 level."  \par \par \uc2 The rupee ended at 45.2400/2450 per dollar on Monday, taking its gains to 0.17 percent over the two sessions since it struck a five-week closing low last Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 Foreign funds have been pouring money into local shares amid cracking economic growth that is lifting demand for goods and services across sectors.  \par \par \uc2 Company stake sales by the government, including an ongoing 10 percent offer in India's most valuable firm, Oil and Natural Gas Corp (ONGC.BO), is also increasing inward investment flows.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said sentiment in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was being supported this week by the dollar's slide back into a trough overseas against most major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , led by the euro, following disappointment over U.S. jobs data last Friday.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  --------