\par\par \b \uc2 India rupee snaps two-day fall, dlr forwards rise.  \b0\par\par\uc2 422 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 12:16\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     BOMBAY, March 5 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee snapped a\par two-day fall against the greenback to end firmer on Friday as a\par cash dollar shortage ebbed and demand from importers of defence\par equipment and state-run oil companies eased, dealers said.\par     The rupee          ended at 45.2900/2950 per dollar, off an\par intra-day low of 45.3400 hit in the morning and firmer than\par Thursday's five-week closing low of 45.3125/3200.\par     Dollar inflows from Indian companies which raised foreign\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  loans in recent days also lent some strength to the\par local unit, dealers said, but suspected \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  had capped the gains.\par     "There appeared to be some \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at the 45.30 per\par dollar level," said a trader at a state-run bank. "Once the\par residual dollar demand got over in the morning, the rupee was\par looking to appreciate."\par     Traders said the outlook for the rupee was upbeat on\par expectations foreign funds will invest in a big way in the\par country's biggest share sale.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     India is selling a 10 percent stake in ONGC, India's most\par valuable company, at 680 to 750 rupees a share. It is selling\par nearly 143 million shares for more than $2 billion, and\par overseas demand on the first day of the sale on Friday was\par strong.\par     Earlier in the day, tight liquidity in the cash dollar\par segment caused by persistent \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and the\par rollover of forward contracts by exporters had led banks to buy\par the greenback.\par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 India\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has been \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in recent\par sessions to prevent the rupee from appreciating much. Analysts\par say the dollar-buying spree by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is an effort to\par keep exports competitive and build \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves.\par     Possible \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by state-run banks, which usually act\par on behalf of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , in the near term forwards had\par pushed up premiums, traders said.\par     The one-month forward ended at an annualised premium of\par 0.21 percent, up from Thursday's close of a 0.22 percent\par discount. The six-month forward edged up to finish at 0.38\par percent, up from the previous close of 0.22 percent.\par  \par -------------------------------------------------------------\par                           MARKET SNAPSHOT\par                                  Friday's close Thursday's\par close\par      Bombay Sensitive Index (pts)      5,880.35        5,815.87\par      Indian rupee (/$)             45.2900/2950    45.3125/3200\par      7.37 percent 2014 bond (%)          5.2531          5.2400\par      Call money (%)                   3.75-4.25       4.25/4.50\par      6-mth fwd annualised premium (%)      0.38            0.22\par -------------------------------------------------------------.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040305e035001pf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
