\par\par \b \uc2 India mulls $8.8 bln monetary stabilisation fund.   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Anirban Nag   \par \uc2 583 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Februar 2004\par 11:33\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 BOMBAY, Feb 19 (Reuters) - The Indian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's planned market stabilisation fund, expected to be approved shortly by the government, will be backed by about 400 billion rupees ($8.8 billion) in bonds, a senior official said on Thursday.   \par \par \uc2 The move is meant to temper inflows that have swelled \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  coffers to record levels, just 13 years after the country suffered a balance of payments crisis.   \par \par \uc2 Under the fund, the government would issue the bonds to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which would sell them when necessary to soak up excess money market liquidity, pumped up by the bank's rupee-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , officials say.   \par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials and we are meeting on February 23 to discuss several issues, including the tenor of these bonds," said a senior finance ministry official on Thursday.   \par \par \uc2 A \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  panel in December proposed the fund in response to the bank's dwindling supply of government bonds - currently the main weapon for sterilisation - which fell to 369 billion rupees last month, from 1.5 trillion rupees in March 2001.   \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would keep the money raised in a separate account that the government would not be able to access, but interest on the bonds would be paid by the government, and analysts say that could weigh on the already huge fiscal deficit.   \par \par \uc2 Analysts say setting up the fund reflects the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's (RBI) resolve to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  more aggressively to cap the rupee's rise against the dollar.   \par \par \uc2 The policy is in line with other Asian central banks that have also been grappling with hefty capital inflows. \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  boosted Asia's combined \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves to more than $2 trillion at end-January.   \par \par \uc2 REINING IN THE RUPEE   \par \par \uc2 The RBI says it does not target a level for the rupee but analysts say it has managed the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a plus-to minus-five percent band around its real effective exchange rate.   \par \par \uc2 "Given that India still has a trade deficit of 3-4 percent of GDP, the RBI would prefer to keep the rupee in the centre to the weaker half of the band by absorbing dollars," said M.R. Madhavan, strategist at Bank of America in Singapore.   \par \par \uc2 "The fund would enable the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to do this more effectively, without any adverse monetary effect."   \par \par \uc2 Sustained \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has swelled \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's reserves, which hit a record $106.6 billion on February 6. In 1998, India was forced to tap expatriates for $4 billion to shore up reserves.   \par \par \uc2 India, home to one-sixth of the world's population, is forecast to see economic growth of over eight percent in the fiscal year to March, after expanding by only four percent a year earlier.   \par \par \uc2 Foreign funds pumped $6.7 billion into Indian shares in 2003, helping fuel a 73 percent surge in stock prices.   \par \par \uc2 The inflows pushed the rupee , which is only partly convertible, 5.2 percent higher against the dollar last year, its steepest gain in a decade. It has strengthened 0.8 percent in 2004.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  helped limit the rupee's rise, but the resultant flood of rupees into domestic markets and three years of soft monetary policy have powered a steep fall in government bond yields.   \par \par \uc2 But with the economy expected to boom, and inflation stubbornly high around six percent, traders expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep rates steady and yields to rise. (Additional reporting by Surojit Gupta in NEW DELHI).    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040219e02j001mq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
