\par\par \b \uc2 Indian bonds ease on market stabilisation fund plan.    \b0\par\par\uc2 387 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Februar 2004\par 05:31\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited    \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     BOMBAY, Feb 10 (Reuters) - Indian government bonds slipped\par on Tuesday morning as investors got ready for a new instrument\par which will sterilise strong foreign capital inflows by mopping\par up some of the excess funds from the money market, dealers\par said.\par  \par     Expectations that interest rates had bottomed out amid\par scorching economic growth also kept traders on the sidelines.\par     The actively traded 8.07 percent bond maturing in 2017 was\par dealt at 5.5622 percent, up from the previous close of 5.5555\par percent.\par     A senior finance ministry official said late on Monday that\par the government would launch a market stabilisation fund in the\par current fiscal year to March 2004.\par     D. Swarup, expenditure secretary, said the fund would allow\par     \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 the government to issue bonds to the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , which would    \f28 \par \par \uc2 float them in the market to suck out excess liquidity whenever    \par \par \uc2 required.    \par \par \uc2 A \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  source told Reuters the bonds could be    \par \par \uc2 floated within 10 or 15 days after the government gives final    \par \par \uc2 approval for the launching of the fund.    \par \par \uc2 For details, please    \par \par \uc2 Traders expect the bonds issued under the fund to absorb    \par \par \uc2 most of the surplus cash in the system. Despite stubbornly high    \par \par \uc2 inflation, bonds have been supported of late by excess cash of    \par \par \uc2 around 300-350 billion rupees in the market coming from \b \highlight22\uc2 central    \b0 \highlight\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 bank foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .    \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's wholesale price inflation has been sticky above the    \par \par \uc2 six percent mark since the start of 2004 on firm prices of    \par \par \uc2 manufactured goods like steel and cement, fuel and food items.    \par \par \uc2 Wholesale price inflation fell marginally to 6.12 percent    \par \par \uc2 in the week to January 24, against a median forecast of 6.0    \par \par \uc2 percent in a Reuters poll and 6.13 percent in the previous    \par \par \uc2 week.    \par \par \uc2 Most traders expect inflation in the week ended January 31    \par \par \uc2 to ease below the six percent mark due to a high base effect,    \par \par \uc2 which is likely to check a sharp fall in bond prices in the    \par \par \uc2 near term.     \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par -------------------------------------------------------------\par                                          MARKET SNAPSHOT\par                                      9:35 a.m.  Monday's close\par     Bombay Sensitive Index (pts)     yet to open      5,926.22\par     Indian rupee (/$)            45.2400/2475     45.2400/2450\par     7.37 percent 2014 bond (%)        5.2149            5.2172\par     Call money (%)                  4.25/4.50        4.25/4.50\par     6-mth fwd annualised premium (%) yet to open          0.51\par -------------------------------------------------------------.\par     \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040210e02a000mb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
