\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-India's  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2  reserves cross $100 billion.   \b0\par\par\uc2 614 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Dezember 2003\par 14:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 NEW DELHI, Dec 20 (Reuters) - India's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves crossed the $100 billion mark for the first time, boosted by foreign investment inflows, trade flows and revaluation gains on the dollar's weakness against global \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 "The governor RBI informed me today that India's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves comprising foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  assets, gold and Special Drawing Rights have reached $100.248 billion on December 19," Finance Minister Jaswant Singh said in a statement. "The RBI has assured me that these \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves will, as in the past, continue to be used to contain volatility in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and to withstand any possible supply shocks."   \par \par \uc2 The reserves will also enable Indian firms to borrow overseas at "finer and more favourable rates", Singh added.   \par \par \uc2 Earlier on Saturday, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  data showed India's reserves climbed $1.44 billion in the week to December 12, notching their seventh straight week of gains and extending rises over the past year to $30.5 billion, or nearly 45 percent.   \par \par \uc2 "The entire rise may not be attributable to money flows, part of it is due to revaluation as the dollar has been depreciating against other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Siddharth Mathur of JP Morgan.   \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said the numbers included the effect of appreciation or depreciation of non-dollar \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  held in the reserves such as the euro, sterling and yen.   \par \par \uc2 The euro has appreciated roughly 19 percent against the dollar this year and hit record highs against the greenback this week. The sterling and the yen are up around 10 percent each against the dollar this year.   \par \par \uc2 Finance Secretary D.C. Gupta told reporters there was "no reason that the government should not continue with its policy of paying foreign debt ahead of schedule but needed to examine whether it would save on interest".   \par \par \uc2 On Friday, an official said the country would pre-pay $1.3 billion to the Asian Development Bank before end-March.   \par \par \uc2 A senior government official said earlier this year that India would pre-pay external debt worth nearly $3.0 billion to more than a dozen countries.   \par \par \uc2 PROBLEM OF PLENTY   \par \par \uc2 Grappling with a problem of plenty, the government earlier this year also tightened its policy on foreign borrowing by Indian firms in what analysts saw as a move to stem inflows.   \par \par \uc2 Earlier this week, global rating agency Standard and Poor's raised its outlook on the country's foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rating to stable from negative, acknowledging the improvement in reserves, although it kept the rating at a speculative 'BB'.   \par \par \uc2 India is placed sixth in Asia in terms of reserves and its reserves are equivalent to around 20 percent of its GDP.   \par \par \uc2 The rise in reserves, besides the revaluation gains, reflects heavy \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the central Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2  to mop up inflows from foreign investors, remittances from Indians working overseas and trade flows.   \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is aimed at preventing excessive rises in the rupee, which would affect \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's export competitiveness. Despite the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the rupee has risen by around 5.4 percent against the dollar in 2003.   \par \par \uc2 Foreign funds have made net investments of some $7.1 billion in Indian assets in 2003, their highest annual investment since local markets were opened to foreign funds a decade ago, on expectations Asia's third-largest economy will expand by over seven percent in the current fiscal year to March.   \par \par \uc2 Deposits by non-resident Indians rose by $2.6 billion in the first half of the fiscal year which started in April but are expected to have grown more slowly since as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cut rates on such deposits in September.    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031220dzck007vd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
