\par\par \b \uc2 Indian rupee at 5-wk closing high on foreign funds.  \b0\par\par\uc2 482 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Dezember 2003\par 12:40\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     BOMBAY, Dec 19 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee eked out gains\par to hit a five-week closing high on Friday as heavy foreign fund\par inflows outstripped dollar purchases by state-run banks\par suspected to be acting on behalf of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , traders\par said.\par     The suspected \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will further shore up \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign\par exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves which are heading towards the $100 billion\par mark, they said.\par      The rupee          ended at 45.5050/5150 per dollar,\par compared with its previous close of 45.5100/5175 and just off a\par psychological level of 45.50. It last finished higher at\par 45.4150/4250 on November 14.\par     It has risen just 0.09 percent during the week, with\par traders attributing the muted gain to the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and sporadic purchases by state-run companies.\par     "They (the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ) are going to keep \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as\par they might be targeting reserves of $100 billion," said a chief\par dealer at a foreign bank.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  India's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves\par were at a record high of $97.5 billion on December 5, according\par to latest \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  data.\par     "They should announce some relaxations on the capital\par account to take make the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  more convertible," the dealer\par added. The rupee is only partially convertible on the capital\par account.\par      \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 India\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  often \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervenes\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  through state-run\par banks to smoothen market volatility. Traders say it has bought\par dollars heavily this year to check the rupee's rise as a strong\par local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  affects the competitiveness of Indian exports.\par     The rupee is up 5.4 percent against the dollar so far in\par 2003, supported by foreign fund investments, remittances from\par Indians working overseas and trade flows. The dollar's weakness\par against global \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has also helped the rupee.\par     Foreign funds have pumped in $7.1 billion so far in 2003\par into local assets, their highest addition in a single year\par since they were allowed to invest in India a decade ago, amid\par expectations that the economy will expand at around seven\par percent this year.\par     Near term discounts on the forward dollar widened as banks\par conducted buy-sell swaps to tide over an expected cash dollar\par crunch at the month-end while some exporters encashed\par receivables, dealers said.\par     The one-month forward ended at an annualised discount of\par 0.60 percent, down from the previous close of 0.42 percent and\par dragging down the premiums on the far forwards.\par  \par     The six-month forward ended at 0.05 percent, down from the\par previous close of 0.12 percent.\par -------------------------------------------------------------\par                                      MARKET SNAPSHOT\par                                Friday's close  Thursday's close\par 