\par\par \b \uc2 Indian rupee ends higher, unfazed by trade data.  \b0\par\par\uc2 372 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Dezember 2003\par 12:47\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     BOMBAY, Dec 2 (Reuters) - The rupee          inched up for\par the second straight day on Tuesday helped by rising foreign\par fund purchases in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 India\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's booming stock markets, but possible\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  capped some of the gains,\par dealers said.\par     Foreign fund inflows helped offset impact from data which\par showed India's trade deficit in October widened to $2.02\par billion from $940 million in the year earlier period.\par     The rupee ended at 45.6900/7000 per dollar, up from\par Monday's 45.7275/7325. It is up five percent since the start of\par the calendar year and analysts said it is undervalued by nearly\par one percent on a trade-weighted basis, implying more gains\par ahead.\par     "There was some support (for the dollar) at 45.70. But\par dollar supplies, possibly from foreign portfolio investors,\par were strong," said a dealer at a large state-run bank.\par     The rupee has gained mainly from foreign portfolio\par investments, drawn by surging stockmarkets and a powerful\par economic growth.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     India's most widely tracked share index rose for a fifth\par straight day on Tuesday to end at a fresh 43-month closing\par high. The Bombay index  which is one of Asia's top\par performer in 2003 with gains of about 50 percent has been\par driven up mainly by foreign fund investments.\par     Foreign funds have pumped in a little more than $6 billion\par so far in 2003, mostly in equities, up from about $740 million\par last year.\par     Robust dollar inflows helped lower discounts on the forward\par dollar towards close, but traders said a bullish outlook for\par the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and tight cash dollar liquidity due to\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would push discounts higher in the\par near term.\par     The annualised discount on the six-month forward opened at\par 0.32 percent, compared to Monday's 0.20 percent, but closed at\par 0.18 percent.\par  \par -------------------------------------------------------------\par                            MARKET SNAPSHOT\par                                 Tuesday's close    Monday's\par close\par     Bombay Sensitive Index (pts)  5,186.08           5,160.85\par     Indian rupee (/$)           45.6900/7000     45.7275/7325\par     7.27 percent 2013 bond (%)       5.2082*            5.2142\par     Call money (%)                 4.25-4.50        4.20/4.40\par     6-mth fwd premium/(discount)    (0.18)             (0.20)\par     * yet to close\par -------------------------------------------------------------.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031202dzc20023o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
