\par\par \b \uc2 India rupee at new two-mth low, c.bank checks falls.  \b0\par\par\uc2 458 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 November 2003\par 12:33\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     BOMBAY, Nov 25 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee weakened to a\par new two-month closing low on Tuesday on a bunching up of dollar\par demand ahead of the month-end, with further falls stopped only\par by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dollar sales, traders said.\par     The rupee          ended at 45.9250/9350 per dollar, weaker\par than Monday's finish of 45.8775/8875 which was its previous\par two-month closing low.\par  \par     The rupee last ended around these levels on September 19,\par at 45.9350/9450.\par     State-run banks sold dollars to lift the rupee off a low of\par 45.965. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 India\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  often \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervenes\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  through state-run\par banks.\par     News that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 India\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and Pakistan would observe a ceasefire in\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Kashmir from midnight had little impact on sentiment in the  \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. Equity traders said the development would be  \par \par \uc2 viewed positively by foreign investors.  \par \par \uc2 "Dollars have been in short supply as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was  \par \par \uc2 buying in the spot market earlier while exporters were mostly  \par \par \uc2 selling in the forward market," said a trader at a state-run  \par \par \uc2 bank.  \par \par \uc2 "Month-end corporate demand and a recent slowdown in  \par \par \uc2 foreign fund investments have added to the pressure on the  \par \par \uc2 rupee," he added.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     Dollar demand usually picks up at the month end, when\par several firms make payments on their debt and imports.\par     Portfolio investment inflows, which were strong earlier in\par the year, have slowed down and are expected to remain subdued\par till end December, as foreign money mangers remit profits.\par     Foreign funds' daily net investments in Indian assets\par averaged $44.4 million this month till November 21, compared\par with $65.1 million in October.\par     They have invested $6 billion in Indian assets this year,\par mostly in shares, helping power a six percent rise in the rupee\par between January 1 and November 7.\par     The rupee has lost 1.42 percent since November 7.\par     Traders were confident the rupee's weakness was temporary\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 as foreign funds were expected to step up their investments  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 next year on a bright outlook for the economy. For a detailed  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 story,  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 That outlook was reflected in the forward market where  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 exporters continued to sell while importers held back from  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 hedging.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The discount on the one-month dollar shot up to 3.31  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 percent from 1.93 percent on Monday, while that on the  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 six-month dollar jumped to 0.31 percent from 0.05 percent.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par -------------------------------------------------------------\par                            MARKET SNAPSHOT\par                                  Tuesday's close   Monday's\par close\par     Bombay Sensitive Index (pts)        4,894.59\par 4,822.15\par     Indian rupee (/$)               45.9250/9350\par 45.8775/8875\par     7.27 percent 2013 bond (%)            5.1248*\par 5.1092\par     Call money (%)                     4.00/4.25\par 4.00/4.25\par     6-mth fwd premium/(discount)           (0.31)\par (0.05)\par  