\par\par \b \uc2 Indian rupee rebounds from lows, exporters step in.  \b0\par\par\uc2 295 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 November 2003\par 08:29\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 BOMBAY, Nov 21 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee bounced from late Friday morning lows as exporters sold dollars at dips, but underlying sentiment was cautious as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  appeared to be comfortable with a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 At 12:25 p.m. the rupee was quoted at 45.80/81 per dollar, off an intra-day low of 45.97, but still weaker than the previous finish of 45.7650/7750 which was a near two-month closing low.  \par \par \uc2 It gained nearly six percent against the dollar until November 7, but has lost about 1.2 percent since then, mainly due to \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , purchases by importers and foreign fund outflows.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  deputy governor, Rakesh Mohan, told Reuters earlier in the day that the rupee's recent weakness could not be termed volatility, causing it to ease further.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said the statement probably meant the authorities were comfortable with a weaker rupee.  \par \par \uc2 For details, please  \par \par \uc2 Traders said importers had stepped up purchases as many were convinced the rupee could come under pressure amid foreign fund outflows ahead of the year-end and steady \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  despite the greenback's overseas woes.  \par \par \uc2 Premiums on dollar forwards rose, in line with the weakening rupee, dealers said. The six-month forward was at annualised 0.19 percent, up from the previous close of 0.14 percent.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  ----------------------------------------