\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Indian cbank says recent rupee weakness not volatility.   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Chamath Ariyadasa   \par \uc2 448 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 November 2003\par 07:55\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 COLOMBO, Nov 21 (Reuters) - Recent weakness in the Indian rupee cannot be termed volatility, a top Indian central banker said on Friday, causing the rupee to ease to a two-month low.   \par \par \uc2 "We cannot characterise the exchange rate market as having been volatile in the last couple of weeks," Reserve Bank of India Deputy Governor Rakesh Mohan, in Colombo to deliver a lecture, told Reuters.   \par \par \uc2 "The exchange rate is market determined. We would like to see an orderly market," Mohan said.   \par \par \uc2 The rupee fell to 45.92/96 per U.S. dollar after the comments, a level not seen since mid-September. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was trading at 45.88/90 per U.S. dollar before the comments and 45.7650/7750 on Thursday.   \par \par \uc2 Traders said the latest fall was due to Mohan's comments.   \par \par \uc2 "These comments mean the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is comfortable with the weakening rupee," said a dealer at a foreign bank.   \par \par \uc2 "We could see the rupee dipping to test the 46 level soon as foreign fund inflows are also slowing."   \par \par \uc2 The rupee has lost 1.5 percent since November 7. Factors include foreign funds booking profits as the year draws to a close, importer demand and aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 But the rupee is still up 4.5 percent since the start of 2003 on the back of robust foreign investment, export inflows and expatriate remittances.   \par \par \uc2 SOFT AND FLEXIBLE   \par \par \uc2 Mohan also told Reuters that the bank had not changed its stance on the cash reserve ratio and interest rates from that outlined in its mid-year policy review.   \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had said in its review on November 3 that its soft and flexible monetary stance continued.   \par \par \uc2 The bank rate, a benchmark that banks use to price loans, is at a three-decade low of six percent after being chopped two percentage points by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  over the past three years.   \par \par \uc2 The repo rate, at which the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  borrows overnight funds, is at 4.5 percent after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  lowered it in August by half a percentage point.   \par \par \uc2 "There has been no material change in conditions since the policy," Mohan said. "We don't make policy changes on a daily basis."   \par \par \uc2 Bond traders say the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will not cut official rates further amid signs of a local and global economic pick-up. The yield on 10-year federal bonds has risen to 5.1 percent from a historical low of 4.94 percent hit in mid-October.   \par \par \uc2 "The market has been working efficiently in India and the market is responding to objective circumstances," Mohan said.    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031121dzbl001k7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
