\par\par \b \uc2 INTERVIEW: Indian Rupee Fall Vs Dlr Likely Temporary  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Anil Varma  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 487 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 November 2003\par 05:52\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI (Dow Jones)--The Indian rupee is likely to resume its advance despite a correction this week, when it fell 1% against the dollar caused by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , says HSBC \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 's head of treasury Tarun Mehrotri.  \par \par \uc2 Mehrotri believes the rupee will soon overcome its current bout of weakness and resume rising on inflows into the local stock market and the dollar's global weakness. He expects the rupee to test INR44.50 by March-end, a level last reached in May 2000.  \par \par \uc2 The local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has gained 4.5% against the dollar so far in 2003, fell sharply this week on heavy dollar buying by big state-owned banks. It closed at INR45.77 Thursday, sharply weaker than INR45.61 Wednesday and INR45.35 a week ago. This fall raised concerns that the rupee's long spell of strength may be over.  \par \par \uc2 Mehrotri disagrees.  \par \par \uc2 "I don't believe that dollar has bottomed out yet against the rupee," he says. "What we saw this week was probably a clear and concerted effort by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stall the rupee's rise."  \par \par \uc2 The RBI is believed to have been indirectly buying dollars on the spot market in recent months to temper the rupee's gains and protect the country's exports. But an apparent intensifying in the buying since last week has puzzled many in the market.  \par \par \uc2 "The strengthening of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action since may be linked to the government's upcoming $2.9 loan repayment to the Asian Development Bank," suggests Mehrotri. "They can now buy dollars in a big way, knowing that the ADB outflow will neutralize any increase in money supply or \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves the dollar purchases may cause."  \par \par \uc2 Indeed, the RBI probably refrained from making big \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the spot market in recent months due to concerns over the possible side-effects - such as higher money supply and inflation - that could jeopardize its soft monetary stance.  \par \par \uc2 Mehrotri believes a recent slowdown in foreign capital inflows into the local stock market, which has added to the rupee's current weakness, is temporary.  \par \par \uc2 Foreign portfolio investments in India, a key booster for the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent months, totaled $664 million so far this month, compared with $1.43 billion during the whole of October.  \par \par \uc2 But, says Mehrotri, "there are bound to be swings in capital flows, it's natural. The outlook is still quite bullish for Indian stocks, so foreign investors should stay."  \par \par \uc2 Furthermore, the weak global outlook for the dollar should continue to favor the rupee, he says.  \par \par \uc2 "The U.S. trade gap continues to be a concern for the dollar," says Mehrotri, who expects the dollar to weaken further against other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the coming months.  \par \par \uc2 -By Anil Varma, Dow Jones Newswires; 91 22 22884212; anil.varma@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Alastair McIndoe  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020031121dzbl0006j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
