\par\par \b \uc2 Indian rupee gives up gains, ends weak on cbank.  \b0\par\par\uc2 380 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 November 2003\par 12:36\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     BOMBAY, Nov 10 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee ended weaker on\par Monday after being brought of an early peak by state-run banks\par which absorbed most of the strong foreign investment and trade\par inflows, possibly on behalf of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , dealers said.\par     The local unit          closed at 45.2850/2900 per dollar,\par off an intra-day high of 45.23, and against Friday's\par 45.2750/2775, which was the highest close in a month.\par     "Dollar inflows were substantial, some of it could have\par been foreign fund investments," said a trader at a state-run\par bank.\par     "But one state-run bank bid the rupee down from 45.23 to\par 45.28. With the dollar not showing much strength overseas, the\par rupee is expected to rise."\par     Foreign fund investments in India have surged this year on\par the back of a tantalising seven percent GDP growth forecast for\par the fiscal year to March 2004.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     These funds have pumped in some $4.92 billion into shares\par so far this year, nearly seven times the total for 2002,\par contributing in some measure to the rupee's rise of six percent\par in the face of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par     Aggressive dollar purchases by the Reserve Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 India\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par have swelled \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves by some $22 billion so\par far in 2003, taking them to a record $92.6 billion on October\par 31.\par     In the forward market, the dollar found its value eroded\par amid concerns that traders may roll over buy-sell swap\par transactions if the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  leaves the\par market short of dollars later this week, when several contracts\par mature.\par     The one-month dollar was quoted at an annualised discount\par of 0.05 percent by close of trade, against a premium of 0.69\par percent on Friday.\par  \par    The six-month dollar was quoted at a premium of 0.41\par percent, 40 basis points below Friday's 0.81 percent.\par -------------------------------------------------------------\par                              MARKET SNAPSHOT\par                                Monday's close      Friday's\par close\par     Bombay Sensitive Index (pts)     4,998.57\par 4,971.57\par     Indian rupee (/$)            45.2850/2900\par 45.2750/2775\par     7.27 percent 2013 bond (%)         5.0677*\par 5.0630\par     Call money (%)                  4.40/4.50\par 4.40/4.50\par     6-mth fwd premium                    0.41\par 0.81\par     * Yet to close\par -------------------------------------------------------------.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031110dzba001cc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
