\par\par \b \uc2 Indian rupee firms on strong trade, fund inflows.  \b0\par\par\uc2 300 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 November 2003\par 05:18\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  BOMBAY, Nov 6 (Reuters) - The Indian rupee rose in early trade on Thursday amid robust dollar selling by exporters and expectations of fresh supplies from foreign funds investing in the booming domestic stock markets.   \par \par \uc2  At 9:30 a.m. (0400 GMT), the rupee  was at 45.2600/2700 per dollar, up from Wednesday's close of 45.28/29.   \par \par \uc2  Traders said the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was expected to trade in a band between 45.25 and 45.30. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be eyed for possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the rupee rose sharply, they said.   \par \par \uc2  "There is some real strong dollar selling in the market and unless the RBI (Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 India\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  a test of 45.25 is possible," a dealer at a state-run bank said.   \par \par \uc2  The rupee has gained six percent against the dollar since the start of 2003 on the back of surging foreign fund investments into Indian stocks and trade and expatriate remittances.   \par \par \uc2  Overseas investors' net purchases of Indian shares and debt in the first two trading days of November stood at $335.5 million, taking the year's total to $5.8 billion. They had invested a net $743.6 million in 2002.   \par \par \uc2  Traders said easing fears of a cash dollar shortage on the back of anticipated infusions by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was also keeping dollar selling sentiment supported.   \par \par \uc2  Demand for cash dollars is expected to spike on Friday when several forward contracts mature.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  --------------------